00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.870
So quick recap, we talked
about a lot of things.

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:07.260
So this is just a summary of
what you will need really today.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:08.850
We talked about Markov chains.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:12.990
So if you remember Markov chains
or Markov processes for a given

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:15.120
k, a k-th order
Markov process means

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:19.170
that you need the previous
k symbols to predict

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:20.760
the next symbol but no more.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:24.750
So looking at further
past doesn't help you.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:28.890
And in particular, a first
order Markov processes,

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:31.800
the current step depends
on the past step.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.950
But given the past step, it
doesn't depend on anything

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.680
before that.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.080
Stationary processes are
like a generalization.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.290
They are any process which is
time stationary, time invariant,

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:47.490
which means that the statistics
don't change with time.

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.950
We mostly will talk about
stationary processes

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.620
only in the context of
certain theoretical results.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:55.870
But in practice, I
guess, just think

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:59.860
of a process given the past,
you can predict something

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:00.640
about the future.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:04.210
So that's the process in a way.

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:10.000
We talked about
conditional entropy, which

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.420
answers the question, you
have two random variables

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:17.020
u and v. And given v,
how much uncertainty

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:20.140
is left in u on average?

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:23.890
And then we talked about
this specific one, u given

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:26.260
v equal to v, which
is given you know

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:29.770
that your random
variable v is equal to v,

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:34.150
how much uncertainty
is left in u.

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:36.370
Or you can talk in
terms of bits used.

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:39.520
So this one says if I
know v, how many bits

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:41.440
do I need to compress u?

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:45.070
And this one says if I know v
equal to v, then how many bits

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:46.255
do I need to compress u.

00:02:49.645 --> 00:02:51.270
And when we looked
at a few properties.

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.820
We looked at the chain
rule, if you remember

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:57.900
where we had the
joint entropy of u

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:03.790
and v is simply the entropy of
v less the entropy of u given

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:10.150
v. We looked at the inequality
that conditioning reduces

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:11.900
entropy.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:14.180
So just some results
we saw, which

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:16.670
you will use in your homework.

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:18.650
And then we talked
about the limit.

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:20.480
So you have a
stationary process.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:24.200
And what you can do is, you
take your n plus 1 symbol,

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:27.140
you condition it
on its entire past,

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:29.420
and then you keep increasing n.

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:31.460
So in the limit
what will happen is

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:34.670
you are trying to capture
given I know the entire past,

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:37.430
is there still some uncertainty
left in the next symbol?

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:40.340
Is each new symbol
adding some entropy,

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:41.900
some randomness, some bits?

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:45.620
And the limit is called
the entropy rate.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:50.060
Another equivalent way
of expressing it is you

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:51.530
think in terms of a block.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:54.770
So you, say, if I take
the first n symbols

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:58.520
and I take the average entropy
and then you keep increasing n

00:03:58.520 --> 00:03:59.990
and then again you
take the limit.

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:04.830
And obviously if you
have done limits,

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:07.650
you will know that this will be
at the end of the day dominated

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:10.320
by the large terms.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:12.820
The first few
terms don't matter.

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:17.279
So it's not very hard to prove
that these two are equal.

00:04:17.279 --> 00:04:20.100
You just need to use
stationarity and some properties

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:25.280
about limits that you might
have seen in calculus.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:28.502
But you don't need
that for this class.

00:04:28.502 --> 00:04:29.960
So with that in
mind, let's quickly

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:32.480
do the homework
which will refresh

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:36.350
our concepts about conditional
entropy, entropy rate,

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:36.980
and so on.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:38.780
And then we'll move on.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:45.130
So we are given a Markov
chain in this question.

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:48.490
We don't say that it is a
stationary Markov chain for now.

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:49.900
So how is it described?

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:51.895
We say U1 is Bernoulli 1/2.

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:58.220
And let me draw it in this
diagram way, which is easier,

00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:00.480
I think, to understand.

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:06.405
So if U1 is 0, we say
that U2 has to be 1.

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:11.420
Is that clear?

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:13.640
Basically, we are
saying that if U1 is 0,

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:16.460
then there is no chance
that U2 is also 0

00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:20.300
because this term is 0.

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:24.020
And if U1 is 1,
then the next symbol

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:27.680
is either 1 or 0 with equal
probability, this sort of thing.

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:29.360
This is 0.5.

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:31.760
This is 0.5.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:35.750
So the first question we
ask is, what is H of U1?

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:42.520
Anyone wants to guess
the entropy of U1.

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:44.680
This doesn't even
need the classroom.

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:47.830
I told you U1 is Bernoulli 1/2.

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:51.100
So the entropy of
U1 is just 1 bit

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:53.230
because it's a Bernoulli
1/2 random variable.

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:57.025
That's good.

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:09.600
Now I ask you, what
is entropy of U2?

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:13.650
There was a question on ed I
saw where U2 is dependent on U1.

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:16.752
So what do you mean
by entropy of U2?

00:06:16.752 --> 00:06:18.210
At the end of the
day, all of these

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:21.420
are random variables, whether or
not they depend on anybody else.

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:23.340
So any random variable
you can compute

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:26.640
its marginal distribution,
you can compute its entropy.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:28.560
Just follow the formulas.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:35.790
So what is, for example,
what is P U2 equal to 0?

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:39.240
So we did this last time, so
I won't spend too much time

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.200
unless anybody has questions.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:43.290
So you can divide
it into two cases.

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:48.290
You do either U1
was 0 or U1 was 1.

00:06:48.290 --> 00:06:50.255
So you can just split
it into two cases.

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:55.845
And then you can
substitute the things.

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:02.410
So 1/2 times 0, 1/2 times 1.

00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:03.410
So I guess this is half.

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:06.820
No.

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:14.500
So U1 is 1/2 2/2.

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:15.000
All right.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:16.560
This term is 0.5.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:17.580
So this is 1/2.

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:18.900
This is 1/2.

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:20.190
This is 1/2.

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:21.750
This is 0.

00:07:21.750 --> 00:07:23.390
So I guess you get 1 by 4.

00:07:23.390 --> 00:07:24.390
Is that what people got?

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:24.930
OK.

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:26.100
Good.

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:29.580
And then you can compute P
U2 equal, which is just 1

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:30.570
minus this thing.

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:31.920
So 3 by 4.

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:33.480
So you can compute the entropy.

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:36.825
This is just the entropy of a
Bernoulli 1/4 random variable.

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:41.755
Anybody remember the answer?

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:48.690
OK.

00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:52.120
I will-- 0.8.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:52.820
0.81.

00:07:52.820 --> 00:07:53.320
OK.

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:53.820
Thank you.

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:54.670
Yes.

00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:55.420
Yes.

00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:59.770
So you have done
this a few times now.

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:01.500
Now is this process stationary?

00:08:09.025 --> 00:08:09.525
Anyone?

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:18.310
I thought it was because
it's a Markov chain,

00:08:18.310 --> 00:08:24.800
and so I guess the
number is stationary

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:26.577
So is a Markov chain
always stationary?

00:08:26.577 --> 00:08:27.410
That's the question.

00:08:27.410 --> 00:08:28.310
Not necessarily.

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:30.020
That's why many times
in the lectures,

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:32.419
we specifically say a
stationary Markov chain.

00:08:32.419 --> 00:08:36.320
In any case, in this
particular example, you see,

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:40.669
we said that stationarity is
a very, very vast property.

00:08:40.669 --> 00:08:42.950
All statistics stay
constant with time.

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:45.680
But in particular, the entropy
should stay constant with time.

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:47.360
And you saw that
the entropy changed.

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:48.720
Entropy of U1 was 1 bit.

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:51.110
Entropy of U2 was 0.81 bits.

00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:53.750
So there is no way
this can be stationary.

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:55.970
A Markov chain is
stationary if you start it

00:08:55.970 --> 00:09:01.340
at a stationary distribution,
which was, I think, question 4.

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:07.790
So this is no because this was
actually because H U2 is not

00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:09.200
equal to H U1.

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:12.110
Is that clear that
this is not stationary?

00:09:12.110 --> 00:09:16.110
This was a-- don't
worry too much about it,

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:22.250
but just to make sure the
concepts we are all on board.

00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:25.760
Then we asked what
is H U2 given U1?

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:27.470
So let's do this quickly.

00:09:27.470 --> 00:09:29.330
U1 equal to 0.

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:34.700
Entropy U2 given U1 equal to 0.

00:09:34.700 --> 00:09:36.410
I think we did an
example last lecture,

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:39.620
so hopefully, we all remember.

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:46.410
So this guy, if U1 equal
to 0, U2 is deterministic.

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:50.280
If U1 is 0, U2 is deterministic.

00:09:53.130 --> 00:10:02.330
And this one is 1 because if U1
is 1 then U2 is Bernoulli 1/2.

00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:04.040
So after you do the
computation, you

00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:07.530
will see that these two
are 1/2, 1/2, obviously,

00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:08.790
so this will be 1/2.

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:18.490
If by any chance you didn't get
these, look at the last lecture,

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:21.940
we did an example, and just
refresh your probability

00:10:21.940 --> 00:10:22.480
textbook.

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:26.225
And I think that's just
following the probability

00:10:26.225 --> 00:10:26.725
formulas.

00:10:29.590 --> 00:10:31.480
And now what we
did was we changed

00:10:31.480 --> 00:10:34.600
the initial distribution,
which does make it stationary.

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:36.490
We didn't ask you to
prove it's stationary.

00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:38.800
You can trust us on that.

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:41.680
And we said now you
compute the entropy rate.

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:43.570
And in the last lecture,
we saw a few things.

00:10:43.570 --> 00:10:49.410
We saw that for IID, the
entropy rate was just

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:51.770
the entropy of the first one.

00:10:51.770 --> 00:10:59.320
For k-th order Markov,
the entropy rate

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:05.560
was just the entropy of Uk plus
1 given all the past symbols.

00:11:05.560 --> 00:11:08.350
So in particular for
a first order Markov,

00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:11.800
this is just going
to be U2 given U1.

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:15.760
The entropy of U2
given the past symbol.

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:20.200
And now we can compute it
using the exact same formula.

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:22.570
So let me try to
copy-paste this one.

00:11:33.830 --> 00:11:38.240
So again, these stay the same.

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:41.600
And now the initial
probability is change.

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:46.125
So I think you will get
2 by 3 as the answer.

00:11:48.990 --> 00:11:51.030
Did people get to 2 by 3?

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:53.300
Yep.

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:53.800
I'm sorry.

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:55.345
Are you asking a question or--

00:11:55.345 --> 00:11:55.960
No.

00:11:55.960 --> 00:11:56.460
Good.

00:12:03.380 --> 00:12:05.330
So that's it.

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:06.290
Yeah.

00:12:06.290 --> 00:12:08.090
Just make yourself
a bit comfortable.

00:12:08.090 --> 00:12:09.258
This is not just this class.

00:12:09.258 --> 00:12:11.300
Generally, I think this
conditional probabilities

00:12:11.300 --> 00:12:13.430
you will come across
in many, many--

00:12:13.430 --> 00:12:15.240
if you do machine
learning, for example,

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:17.540
or any sort of statistics
or probability class.

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:26.370
So moving on to the material,
any questions so far

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:29.670
on the quiz on last lecture?

00:12:29.670 --> 00:12:33.150
So the question is, how do
you achieve the entropy rate?

00:12:33.150 --> 00:12:38.350
And you will realize
it's very, very simple.

00:12:38.350 --> 00:12:40.330
So we will first start
with a very simple case,

00:12:40.330 --> 00:12:42.490
the first order Markov source.

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:45.410
So what is the first
order Markov source again?

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:47.650
The next symbol depends
on the previous symbol.

00:12:47.650 --> 00:12:49.800
That's it.

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:55.450
So now if you recall
the entropy rate

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:57.490
was defined in
two different ways

00:12:57.490 --> 00:12:59.500
when we looked at last time.

00:12:59.500 --> 00:13:01.000
There is this definition.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:03.010
And then there is
this definition.

00:13:03.010 --> 00:13:04.750
And for a first
order Markov source,

00:13:04.750 --> 00:13:07.480
the second one reduces to this.

00:13:07.480 --> 00:13:11.290
So there are basically
two definitions, this one.

00:13:11.290 --> 00:13:15.340
The first one says that we take
the joint entropy of n symbols

00:13:15.340 --> 00:13:17.740
at a time and then
we divide it by n.

00:13:17.740 --> 00:13:20.320
So this just suggests
you do block coding.

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:23.800
You just take n symbols at
a time, code them together,

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:25.600
and as you take bigger
and bigger blocks,

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:27.850
you are going to achieve
the entropy rate just

00:13:27.850 --> 00:13:29.613
by definition of this thing.

00:13:29.613 --> 00:13:31.780
And we know how to achieve
the entropy of this thing

00:13:31.780 --> 00:13:37.620
by Huffman coding or
arithmetic, any of these codes.

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:39.335
The other solution
is more interesting,

00:13:39.335 --> 00:13:41.460
and that's the one we'll
do most of the time today,

00:13:41.460 --> 00:13:44.490
is more incremental
approach where you actually

00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:46.410
try to do this conditionally.

00:13:46.410 --> 00:13:49.530
You take U1, you try
to encode U2 given U1.

00:13:49.530 --> 00:13:52.575
And then you try to encode
U3 given U2 and so on.

00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:02.230
So the first one, which
is the block coding

00:14:02.230 --> 00:14:04.960
is, you can just do Huffman
on blocks of length n

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:07.000
and then you make
n bigger and bigger

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:09.880
and it will achieve
the entropy rate.

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:13.023
But it doesn't work very well.

00:14:13.023 --> 00:14:15.190
First of all, you can't
make the block size too big,

00:14:15.190 --> 00:14:18.260
the complexity issues we
talked about last time.

00:14:18.260 --> 00:14:22.190
And there is another
issue, which hopefully you

00:14:22.190 --> 00:14:26.510
can understand, which is that
when you had your usual IID

00:14:26.510 --> 00:14:30.830
symbols, so you had U1,
U2, U3, U4, and let's

00:14:30.830 --> 00:14:32.480
say you were coding in blocks.

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:33.920
You took U1 and U2 together.

00:14:33.920 --> 00:14:37.630
You took U3 and U4 together.

00:14:37.630 --> 00:14:40.180
This was suboptimal because
the Huffman code is only

00:14:40.180 --> 00:14:41.740
optimal for a dyadic source.

00:14:41.740 --> 00:14:44.650
If you have symbols whose
probability is not a power of 2,

00:14:44.650 --> 00:14:47.920
you don't get the perfect
code length and so on.

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:48.880
It's not optimal.

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:51.340
But here there is
another reason why

00:14:51.340 --> 00:14:56.440
it's not optimal is that
U2 and U3 are correlated.

00:14:56.440 --> 00:14:58.630
There is some correlation
between U2 and U3.

00:14:58.630 --> 00:15:01.150
So if you're encoding
them in blocks separately

00:15:01.150 --> 00:15:03.550
you just lose out
on the correlation

00:15:03.550 --> 00:15:06.460
between consecutive symbols.

00:15:06.460 --> 00:15:12.000
So with that in mind,
do this exercise at home

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:15.150
just to convince
yourself of these things.

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:17.280
But let's not do it now.

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:20.870
Anyway, this technique is not
something we are going to use.

00:15:20.870 --> 00:15:22.940
This is the one we
are going to use.

00:15:22.940 --> 00:15:25.430
So if everybody
would pay attention,

00:15:25.430 --> 00:15:27.830
let's recap arithmetic coding.

00:15:27.830 --> 00:15:30.440
This will be crucial for
the rest of this lecture.

00:15:30.440 --> 00:15:34.010
This is all you need to
know about arithmetic coding

00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:36.440
in this lecture and just
in generally, I think,

00:15:36.440 --> 00:15:39.650
unless you're actually
going to implement it.

00:15:39.650 --> 00:15:41.090
So arithmetic coding.

00:15:41.090 --> 00:15:42.440
You work with intervals.

00:15:42.440 --> 00:15:44.510
You divide the intervals
into your symbols.

00:15:44.510 --> 00:15:48.260
And at every step, you split
the sub-interval corresponding

00:15:48.260 --> 00:15:50.090
to your symbol.

00:15:50.090 --> 00:15:55.460
So if you have IID model, the
thing we have already studied,

00:15:55.460 --> 00:15:59.270
and you have 0, 1, 2, if
the first symbol is 2,

00:15:59.270 --> 00:16:01.700
you take the interval
corresponding to 2.

00:16:01.700 --> 00:16:04.820
You split it into
three parts, 0, 1, 2,

00:16:04.820 --> 00:16:08.390
which are proportional
to their probabilities.

00:16:08.390 --> 00:16:10.490
And if the next
symbol is now 0, you

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:12.650
take the interval
corresponding to 0,

00:16:12.650 --> 00:16:15.140
and you again split it into
three parts corresponding

00:16:15.140 --> 00:16:16.100
to 0, 1, 2.

00:16:16.100 --> 00:16:17.630
Both sizes are
again proportional

00:16:17.630 --> 00:16:19.460
to their probabilities.

00:16:19.460 --> 00:16:22.200
And at the end of
this, very easy to see

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:25.170
that the length of the
interval that you get

00:16:25.170 --> 00:16:26.940
at the end of the
encoding is simply

00:16:26.940 --> 00:16:30.840
the product of
the probabilities.

00:16:30.840 --> 00:16:32.850
And the way we did
the encoding in bits

00:16:32.850 --> 00:16:36.060
was that the code length
was roughly logarithm of 1

00:16:36.060 --> 00:16:39.795
by interval length, which was
roughly this log 1 by Px1 Px2

00:16:39.795 --> 00:16:44.060
Pxn sort of thing.

00:16:44.060 --> 00:16:46.460
Let me pause for a minute,
make sure everybody

00:16:46.460 --> 00:16:49.372
understands this because
we'll deal with this

00:16:49.372 --> 00:16:50.330
throughout the lecture.

00:16:53.260 --> 00:16:53.760
All right.

00:16:53.760 --> 00:16:56.790
This is arithmetic coding
in a single slide basically.

00:17:07.359 --> 00:17:08.810
That expected comeback.

00:17:08.810 --> 00:17:09.619
That's good.

00:17:09.619 --> 00:17:11.930
All [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:11.930 --> 00:17:12.530
Yes.

00:17:12.530 --> 00:17:12.890
Yes.

00:17:12.890 --> 00:17:13.160
Yes.

00:17:13.160 --> 00:17:13.660
Yes.

00:17:13.660 --> 00:17:19.700
So this thing is for
the n block basically,

00:17:19.700 --> 00:17:21.095
yeah, the entire block.

00:17:26.030 --> 00:17:28.220
And if you recall
our thumb rule,

00:17:28.220 --> 00:17:30.500
we said that for any
symbol x, we want

00:17:30.500 --> 00:17:32.780
to pay basically log 1 by Px.

00:17:32.780 --> 00:17:35.810
And you see that's exactly what
arithmetic coding is paying.

00:17:35.810 --> 00:17:38.615
It's paying exactly log
1 over P for each symbol.

00:17:41.930 --> 00:17:46.415
So if we are happy with this,
and I hope we are, we will--

00:18:16.590 --> 00:18:18.480
so we'll get this
Markov chain where

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:21.390
every symbol can go
to either itself or 1

00:18:21.390 --> 00:18:25.500
plus that symbol modulo 3.

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:27.190
Look at this figure.

00:18:27.190 --> 00:18:29.700
We change something
in the previous slide.

00:18:29.700 --> 00:18:32.970
We change the arithmetic
coding how it is done.

00:18:32.970 --> 00:18:37.650
I will wait and ask you to tell
me what did we do different now.

00:18:40.550 --> 00:18:43.475
So we are encoding 2, 0,
1 is what we are encoding.

00:18:49.170 --> 00:18:50.300
Let's start first.

00:18:50.300 --> 00:18:52.490
Just for encoding 2, did
we do anything different

00:18:52.490 --> 00:18:55.760
when we are encoding 2 compared
to the good old arithmetic

00:18:55.760 --> 00:18:58.160
coding?

00:18:58.160 --> 00:18:59.300
I can answer that one.

00:18:59.300 --> 00:19:02.630
No, we did not
because we just took--

00:19:02.630 --> 00:19:05.120
we assume the initial thing
is uniformly distributed,

00:19:05.120 --> 00:19:08.660
and we just made three
equal length intervals.

00:19:08.660 --> 00:19:12.800
For the next one, anybody wants
to tell me, what did we do?

00:19:12.800 --> 00:19:15.410
What is different
from the previous one?

00:19:15.410 --> 00:19:16.960
Yep.

00:19:16.960 --> 00:19:21.940
Basically, I think because
2 can never go to 1.

00:19:21.940 --> 00:19:27.790
So we didn't have 1
and then the next one,

00:19:27.790 --> 00:19:33.490
the two in that case was gone
because you cannot get to 2.

00:19:33.490 --> 00:19:34.030
Yeah.

00:19:34.030 --> 00:19:34.390
Yeah.

00:19:34.390 --> 00:19:34.600
Yeah.

00:19:34.600 --> 00:19:35.100
Very good.

00:19:35.100 --> 00:19:36.730
Yes, so what did we change?

00:19:36.730 --> 00:19:40.030
What we changed was if you know
that the first symbol is 2,

00:19:40.030 --> 00:19:41.710
now your probability
model tells you

00:19:41.710 --> 00:19:43.940
that definitely the
second symbol is either 0

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:45.850
or 2 with 1/2 1/2 probability.

00:19:45.850 --> 00:19:50.920
So rather than just following
blindly the old distribution,

00:19:50.920 --> 00:19:53.830
you used a different
distribution here.

00:19:53.830 --> 00:19:57.280
You split the interval according
to the conditional distribution.

00:19:57.280 --> 00:20:00.130
You split the interval
according by using the knowledge

00:20:00.130 --> 00:20:01.690
that the previous symbol is 2.

00:20:01.690 --> 00:20:04.540
And similarly for the next
one, you use the knowledge

00:20:04.540 --> 00:20:05.890
that the previous symbol was 0.

00:20:05.890 --> 00:20:08.710
So therefore, the next
symbol can be either 0 or 1.

00:20:08.710 --> 00:20:11.050
So it just made this
slight simple modification

00:20:11.050 --> 00:20:14.500
in arithmetic coding,
which we will see

00:20:14.500 --> 00:20:16.810
is basically the thing to do.

00:20:21.650 --> 00:20:25.580
One thing, I think, there is a
question in the homework where

00:20:25.580 --> 00:20:31.130
you will see a
similar thing, the one

00:20:31.130 --> 00:20:33.380
where you generate
random variables.

00:20:33.380 --> 00:20:34.850
So just a hint for that.

00:20:38.170 --> 00:20:40.890
So if we are happy with that--

00:20:40.890 --> 00:20:43.870
please let me know if you
are not happy with that.

00:20:43.870 --> 00:20:47.260
If you are not just
trust it's fine.

00:20:47.260 --> 00:20:49.630
You can follow the rest of
the lecture, nothing breaks.

00:20:52.300 --> 00:20:53.800
So we just change the model.

00:20:53.800 --> 00:20:55.780
At every step instead of
splitting the interval

00:20:55.780 --> 00:20:58.450
by just the marginal
probability,

00:20:58.450 --> 00:21:01.060
we split the interval according
to the conditional probability

00:21:01.060 --> 00:21:02.795
given the past.

00:21:02.795 --> 00:21:05.170
And in this case because it's
a first order Markov chain,

00:21:05.170 --> 00:21:06.878
you just need to look
at the last symbol.

00:21:06.878 --> 00:21:10.540
But in general, you can
look at the entire past.

00:21:10.540 --> 00:21:13.180
So whatever knowledge you
have about the distribution,

00:21:13.180 --> 00:21:14.560
you have seen the entire past.

00:21:14.560 --> 00:21:16.390
The next time you
split the intervals,

00:21:16.390 --> 00:21:17.920
you use that knowledge.

00:21:21.170 --> 00:21:23.472
And then you can do
the math, which I won't

00:21:23.472 --> 00:21:24.680
spend too much time on doing.

00:21:24.680 --> 00:21:26.570
But what you will get
at the end of the day

00:21:26.570 --> 00:21:28.640
is that the expected
bits per symbol you get

00:21:28.640 --> 00:21:31.130
is actually equal to the
conditional entropy which is

00:21:31.130 --> 00:21:32.570
the entropy rate in this case.

00:21:35.420 --> 00:21:36.870
Maybe I'll spend a little time.

00:21:36.870 --> 00:21:40.650
So you remember that the length
of the interval for arithmetic

00:21:40.650 --> 00:21:42.935
coding was a product
of the probabilities.

00:21:42.935 --> 00:21:45.060
And in this case, the
product of the probabilities,

00:21:45.060 --> 00:21:47.560
you need to take the conditional
probabilities at every step

00:21:47.560 --> 00:21:50.040
because that's how we
split the intervals.

00:21:50.040 --> 00:21:52.860
And so the bits for
encoding is roughly log of 1

00:21:52.860 --> 00:21:55.260
over the-- everything
else stays the same.

00:21:55.260 --> 00:21:57.750
The way you convert intervals
to bits stays the same.

00:21:57.750 --> 00:22:00.000
Just the way you split
the intervals changes.

00:22:02.930 --> 00:22:03.430
Yeah.

00:22:03.430 --> 00:22:05.050
Then you do the math.

00:22:05.050 --> 00:22:07.030
First time is
special because there

00:22:07.030 --> 00:22:09.700
is no past for the first term,
but every term after that, there

00:22:09.700 --> 00:22:12.410
is a past.

00:22:12.410 --> 00:22:14.230
You use your usual
formulas and then

00:22:14.230 --> 00:22:17.080
you get roughly
entropy of U2 given U1.

00:22:21.640 --> 00:22:23.560
So this is the
generalized version

00:22:23.560 --> 00:22:27.370
of the thing we just saw.

00:22:27.370 --> 00:22:31.110
So this is context
based arithmetic coding.

00:22:31.110 --> 00:22:33.490
This is the core
of today's lecture.

00:22:33.490 --> 00:22:34.870
You have data.

00:22:34.870 --> 00:22:36.190
You have a model.

00:22:36.190 --> 00:22:39.670
The model may or may not be
the true model for the data.

00:22:39.670 --> 00:22:43.080
Your data might come from
a probability distribution.

00:22:43.080 --> 00:22:45.870
Maybe your model data does
not come from a probability

00:22:45.870 --> 00:22:46.800
distribution.

00:22:46.800 --> 00:22:49.140
Just you are given
a sequence and you

00:22:49.140 --> 00:22:50.490
have a probability model.

00:22:50.490 --> 00:22:53.970
You have a way to say that
if my past was A, B, C,

00:22:53.970 --> 00:22:57.120
D then my next symbol
is E with probability

00:22:57.120 --> 00:22:58.840
50%, something like that.

00:22:58.840 --> 00:23:01.350
You have a model like
that, a prediction model.

00:23:04.580 --> 00:23:08.240
First step, you just split your
intervals according to the--

00:23:08.240 --> 00:23:09.740
basically, the
model has no past,

00:23:09.740 --> 00:23:13.400
so you just predict the first
symbol with the probabilities.

00:23:13.400 --> 00:23:16.730
Second step, you have
one previous symbol.

00:23:16.730 --> 00:23:19.490
So you predict the probabilities
according to the past,

00:23:19.490 --> 00:23:21.020
the previous symbol.

00:23:21.020 --> 00:23:26.040
And next step you basically
do the past two symbols,

00:23:26.040 --> 00:23:28.170
and then the past three,
past four, you just

00:23:28.170 --> 00:23:30.070
take the entire
past at every step.

00:23:30.070 --> 00:23:36.120
So in this example,
for example, here you

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:39.540
see, for example, that if I
know the first two symbols are

00:23:39.540 --> 00:23:42.450
2 and 0, my model
thinks that 1 is more

00:23:42.450 --> 00:23:45.510
likely to occur than 0 or 2.

00:23:45.510 --> 00:23:47.460
Is that clear?

00:23:47.460 --> 00:23:49.380
This is what this
model is doing.

00:23:49.380 --> 00:23:51.780
It's saying that if my previous
two symbols are 2 and 0,

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:53.700
then the next symbol
is more likely to be 1

00:23:53.700 --> 00:23:55.530
and less likely to be 0 or 2.

00:23:55.530 --> 00:23:57.390
So it's just splitting
the intervals happily

00:23:57.390 --> 00:24:00.510
according to its
probability prediction.

00:24:03.250 --> 00:24:06.640
The code length will be the log
of the 1 over interval length,

00:24:06.640 --> 00:24:08.770
this didn't change, which
is the log of the product

00:24:08.770 --> 00:24:11.350
of the probabilities.

00:24:11.350 --> 00:24:13.630
And really this is the core.

00:24:13.630 --> 00:24:17.020
Better predictor gives
you higher probabilities.

00:24:17.020 --> 00:24:18.820
So if you have a
very good predictor,

00:24:18.820 --> 00:24:21.190
then the thing you
actually observe

00:24:21.190 --> 00:24:23.320
will have very
high probabilities.

00:24:23.320 --> 00:24:25.030
If you have a good
predictor for English,

00:24:25.030 --> 00:24:27.760
then it will say that after a
Q, you are most likely going

00:24:27.760 --> 00:24:30.580
to get a U. So better
predictor will give you

00:24:30.580 --> 00:24:33.950
higher probabilities for the
thing you actually observe.

00:24:33.950 --> 00:24:36.620
Higher probabilities lead to
larger intervals in arithmetic

00:24:36.620 --> 00:24:38.390
coding because the
interval length is

00:24:38.390 --> 00:24:41.930
a product of the probabilities,
which gives you smaller code

00:24:41.930 --> 00:24:42.860
length.

00:24:42.860 --> 00:24:45.740
So the chain is basically
better prediction gives you

00:24:45.740 --> 00:24:46.640
smaller code length.

00:24:46.640 --> 00:24:51.540
So better prediction gives
you better compression.

00:24:51.540 --> 00:24:53.090
I will again wait
for a second here.

00:24:59.375 --> 00:25:01.250
And we are reaching a
point where you can now

00:25:01.250 --> 00:25:02.690
forget about arithmetic coding.

00:25:02.690 --> 00:25:04.760
Just remember that better
prediction gives you

00:25:04.760 --> 00:25:06.560
better compression,
and that's all

00:25:06.560 --> 00:25:09.500
you need for the rest of
the lecture basically.

00:25:09.500 --> 00:25:13.330
Arithmetic coding is just
a tool to get you there.

00:25:13.330 --> 00:25:14.510
Any questions?

00:25:38.290 --> 00:25:40.270
So this is just the same
thing written in text.

00:25:40.270 --> 00:25:42.460
So as long as we can
estimate the probability

00:25:42.460 --> 00:25:44.980
of the next symbol
given some context.

00:25:44.980 --> 00:25:47.740
So context just means the past.

00:25:47.740 --> 00:25:49.690
Maybe you take the entire
past, maybe you just

00:25:49.690 --> 00:25:50.690
look at the last symbol.

00:25:50.690 --> 00:25:51.275
It depends.

00:25:51.275 --> 00:25:52.660
It depends on the model.

00:25:52.660 --> 00:25:55.030
The model can be anything
as far as we are concerned.

00:25:55.030 --> 00:25:57.490
We will look at some
specific models shortly.

00:25:57.490 --> 00:26:00.790
We can use then arithmetic
coding to encode the data.

00:26:00.790 --> 00:26:06.050
And the number of bits used to
encode U is just log 1 over P

00:26:06.050 --> 00:26:06.620
basically.

00:26:06.620 --> 00:26:09.170
This log rule, the thumb
rule we have been seeing,

00:26:09.170 --> 00:26:10.700
this is what you pay.

00:26:10.700 --> 00:26:12.590
So if you are--

00:26:12.590 --> 00:26:14.360
let's just do it
more specifically.

00:26:14.360 --> 00:26:21.050
So good model will
have P Un given past.

00:26:21.050 --> 00:26:24.770
So if it's a very good model,
then this will be closer to 1.

00:26:24.770 --> 00:26:27.440
It will truly be able to
predict strongly that this

00:26:27.440 --> 00:26:28.880
should be your next thing.

00:26:28.880 --> 00:26:33.340
And so then you pay
basically 0 bits.

00:26:33.340 --> 00:26:35.350
So that's really nice.

00:26:35.350 --> 00:26:41.890
And if it's a bad model,
then the probability--

00:26:41.890 --> 00:26:44.830
a bad model would
say like, after a Q,

00:26:44.830 --> 00:26:49.540
you are never going to get a
U. So it will be very, very

00:26:49.540 --> 00:26:50.890
bad model for English.

00:26:50.890 --> 00:26:55.300
And then log of 1
over very small number

00:26:55.300 --> 00:26:57.940
is going to be large.

00:26:57.940 --> 00:27:01.400
So if you have a good model,
good compression, bad model,

00:27:01.400 --> 00:27:02.540
bad compression.

00:27:05.460 --> 00:27:07.000
This is a fun example.

00:27:07.000 --> 00:27:11.910
So Llama is a language
model released by Meta.

00:27:11.910 --> 00:27:15.780
So I was just trying yesterday.

00:27:15.780 --> 00:27:19.110
If you take a word
and you try to predict

00:27:19.110 --> 00:27:22.407
the next word or a next
token, and what probability

00:27:22.407 --> 00:27:23.490
distribution does it give?

00:27:23.490 --> 00:27:25.890
And I was just looking
at the top five guesses.

00:27:25.890 --> 00:27:28.830
So it looked at "than".

00:27:28.830 --> 00:27:34.110
And the top guess was "thanks",
I guess, in some new lingo.

00:27:34.110 --> 00:27:39.450
But I don't know what thane is.

00:27:39.450 --> 00:27:41.160
I see thanking.

00:27:41.160 --> 00:27:45.660
So we see with 18% probability,
it's thought that T-H-A-N would

00:27:45.660 --> 00:27:49.620
be followed by X.

00:27:49.620 --> 00:27:51.210
Then I gave it more context.

00:27:51.210 --> 00:27:54.990
I said, oh, it's
actually "louder than".

00:27:54.990 --> 00:28:01.080
And now suddenly you see that
louder than words, bombs.

00:28:01.080 --> 00:28:03.570
So words got 30.4%.

00:28:03.570 --> 00:28:05.092
So I gave it more
context and you

00:28:05.092 --> 00:28:06.300
see the distribution changed.

00:28:06.300 --> 00:28:07.920
Now it's thinks
something different

00:28:07.920 --> 00:28:10.590
about the next next word.

00:28:10.590 --> 00:28:13.080
Then I said "speak louder than".

00:28:13.080 --> 00:28:15.510
So you might already
guess where we are going.

00:28:15.510 --> 00:28:17.580
And I got words.

00:28:17.580 --> 00:28:21.000
Words is increasing now,
close to 50% probability.

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:31.250
So in this case, if words
is the true next symbol,

00:28:31.250 --> 00:28:33.000
can somebody tell me
how many bits would I

00:28:33.000 --> 00:28:35.610
pay to encode words
with this model, roughly

00:28:35.610 --> 00:28:37.470
how many bits would I pay?

00:28:37.470 --> 00:28:39.190
Yeah, I see one.

00:28:39.190 --> 00:28:39.690
Why?

00:28:39.690 --> 00:28:43.870
Because log of 1 over 1 over 2,
1/2 is roughly the probability

00:28:43.870 --> 00:28:44.370
here.

00:28:44.370 --> 00:28:46.600
This is 1 bit.

00:28:46.600 --> 00:28:49.030
So I'm at a point where
given the past three words,

00:28:49.030 --> 00:28:52.900
I'm able to encode the next
word, a word if you just

00:28:52.900 --> 00:28:55.990
naively encode it would be
a lot of bits to encode.

00:28:55.990 --> 00:29:00.430
You're just able to encode it
with one bit at this point.

00:29:00.430 --> 00:29:02.410
And then I just
basically gave it

00:29:02.410 --> 00:29:04.480
the game like, "Actions
speak louder than"

00:29:04.480 --> 00:29:07.420
and now you see word is 96.5%.

00:29:07.420 --> 00:29:15.930
It can be now encoded
with far fewer than 1 bit.

00:29:15.930 --> 00:29:17.010
So what did we learn?

00:29:17.010 --> 00:29:21.150
We learned that the more
context you give to a model,

00:29:21.150 --> 00:29:23.280
if it's a good model,
it's going to be

00:29:23.280 --> 00:29:26.970
able to predict the next symbol
with more and more confidence

00:29:26.970 --> 00:29:30.930
and therefore it will incur
less bits when you try

00:29:30.930 --> 00:29:32.130
to compress with that model.

00:29:35.350 --> 00:29:37.600
Obviously, we can do this
because these models are now

00:29:37.600 --> 00:29:38.140
very good.

00:29:38.140 --> 00:29:43.340
If you take a silly model from
20 years ago, just give it long,

00:29:43.340 --> 00:29:44.695
it won't do anything useful.

00:29:48.650 --> 00:29:51.590
Then I tried this, just
another simple example,

00:29:51.590 --> 00:29:54.650
I tried "Stanford's
data compression".

00:29:54.650 --> 00:29:59.930
And it suggested research,
group, library, team.

00:29:59.930 --> 00:30:01.370
Class was nowhere there.

00:30:01.370 --> 00:30:04.160
Course, class, very sad.

00:30:04.160 --> 00:30:08.180
But then I said "Enrolling in
Stanford's data compression",

00:30:08.180 --> 00:30:09.740
and see how the
distribution changes.

00:30:09.740 --> 00:30:11.990
Course, class, program, so on.

00:30:11.990 --> 00:30:15.565
So the longer context
to give, usually it

00:30:15.565 --> 00:30:16.940
will give you a
better prediction

00:30:16.940 --> 00:30:18.800
if you're using
a powerful model.

00:30:18.800 --> 00:30:20.135
So that's the lesson.

00:30:23.230 --> 00:30:26.530
So if you know that your data
comes from a k-th order Markov

00:30:26.530 --> 00:30:28.930
model, then the
previous k symbols

00:30:28.930 --> 00:30:32.020
are sufficient to
predict the next symbol.

00:30:32.020 --> 00:30:34.720
If it's a Markov model, then you
only need the previous symbol.

00:30:34.720 --> 00:30:36.550
You don't need
this long history.

00:30:36.550 --> 00:30:40.150
But in general, for real life
data, it's not a Markov model.

00:30:40.150 --> 00:30:43.900
It's not a k-th order
Markov model for any k.

00:30:43.900 --> 00:30:48.160
If you think, you're reading
a novel, for example,

00:30:48.160 --> 00:30:52.340
and some character occurs
in the first chapter.

00:30:52.340 --> 00:30:56.000
And then they might not occur
for the last five chapters

00:30:56.000 --> 00:30:58.110
and then suddenly occur again.

00:30:58.110 --> 00:31:01.520
So then actually having the
context of the past six chapters

00:31:01.520 --> 00:31:04.040
is important to be able to
guess the name of that character

00:31:04.040 --> 00:31:05.510
in the last chapter.

00:31:05.510 --> 00:31:09.350
So there is a limit.

00:31:09.350 --> 00:31:12.530
Computationally, you can't just
look at everything in the past,

00:31:12.530 --> 00:31:14.060
but our brain does
that in a way.

00:31:14.060 --> 00:31:16.310
When you're reading a novel
even if something happened

00:31:16.310 --> 00:31:18.590
in the past, I guess,
we flip back the pages

00:31:18.590 --> 00:31:20.870
and look if you don't remember.

00:31:26.450 --> 00:31:29.810
We will come back to some
fun things a bit later.

00:31:29.810 --> 00:31:32.600
Again, back to the grind.

00:31:32.600 --> 00:31:34.220
Block diagrams.

00:31:34.220 --> 00:31:37.940
So everything we
just said can be

00:31:37.940 --> 00:31:42.170
summarized in this figure
where you have a context.

00:31:42.170 --> 00:31:43.730
Context means past.

00:31:43.730 --> 00:31:45.200
You have a model.

00:31:45.200 --> 00:31:48.230
Given the model--
given the context,

00:31:48.230 --> 00:31:50.840
the model predicts the
probability of the next symbol.

00:31:50.840 --> 00:31:53.870
And then the arithmetic
coder is able to encode

00:31:53.870 --> 00:31:59.160
it using these many
bits for each symbol.

00:31:59.160 --> 00:32:01.470
And therefore the total
bits you use for encoding is

00:32:01.470 --> 00:32:06.420
this the sum of the log loss.

00:32:06.420 --> 00:32:08.730
Anybody tell me, how
would the decoding work?

00:32:08.730 --> 00:32:12.390
Can the decoder-- what would
a decoder block diagram

00:32:12.390 --> 00:32:13.545
look like for this thing?

00:32:22.787 --> 00:32:24.120
Does the decoder need the model?

00:32:29.540 --> 00:32:30.680
Yeah, I'm seeing some nods.

00:32:30.680 --> 00:32:34.370
So basically, you remember
that arithmetic decoding

00:32:34.370 --> 00:32:37.130
is very symmetric to
arithmetic encoding.

00:32:37.130 --> 00:32:40.370
The decoder also needs to know
how to split the intervals.

00:32:40.370 --> 00:32:44.480
So the decoder needs the
same model basically.

00:32:44.480 --> 00:32:46.130
Decoder needs to
use the same model.

00:32:46.130 --> 00:32:48.830
And at step i, it
will have the past.

00:32:48.830 --> 00:32:51.290
So decoder will sort
of work step by step.

00:32:51.290 --> 00:32:54.440
At step 0, it has nothing
but it has the model,

00:32:54.440 --> 00:32:56.480
so it's able to make
those intervals.

00:32:56.480 --> 00:32:58.820
It decodes U1.

00:32:58.820 --> 00:33:00.170
Then it knows U1.

00:33:00.170 --> 00:33:02.150
It feeds U1 into the model.

00:33:02.150 --> 00:33:06.170
It gets the next step
probabilities, decodes U2,

00:33:06.170 --> 00:33:06.930
and so on.

00:33:06.930 --> 00:33:11.690
So this is iteratively
able to decode.

00:33:11.690 --> 00:33:14.580
Is that somewhat clear?

00:33:14.580 --> 00:33:17.160
And we'll look at some
specific models which hopefully

00:33:17.160 --> 00:33:19.732
will make it slightly clearer.

00:33:19.732 --> 00:33:21.315
And you will have a
homework question.

00:33:27.630 --> 00:33:31.035
So really very symmetric, if we
wanted to-- maybe let's just--

00:33:36.340 --> 00:33:38.310
let's try to draw it.

00:33:41.420 --> 00:33:54.380
So the decoder, again, it has
the past U1 want to Ui minus 1.

00:33:54.380 --> 00:33:55.310
It has the model.

00:33:59.320 --> 00:34:00.940
Model gives you the probability.

00:34:04.020 --> 00:34:06.409
You do a decoding.

00:34:06.409 --> 00:34:08.159
Obviously, there is
another input, the bit

00:34:08.159 --> 00:34:11.960
array, the compressed--

00:34:11.960 --> 00:34:14.810
obviously, it uses
the compressed bits.

00:34:14.810 --> 00:34:18.060
And it then decodes Ui.

00:34:18.060 --> 00:34:23.460
And then this Ui becomes
your past basically.

00:34:23.460 --> 00:34:24.750
It goes to your past.

00:34:24.750 --> 00:34:27.540
So it's this decoding where
the decoder every step

00:34:27.540 --> 00:34:30.270
it decodes one symbol,
that goes into the past.

00:34:30.270 --> 00:34:33.389
Then it predicts the next
symbol, decodes the next symbol,

00:34:33.389 --> 00:34:34.394
then so on.

00:34:40.150 --> 00:34:42.730
If you make a compressor, make
sure to write a decompressor

00:34:42.730 --> 00:34:43.449
also.

00:34:43.449 --> 00:34:48.270
Some of these things, you can
get compression of 0 bits,

00:34:48.270 --> 00:34:51.330
everything is good, but
then you can't decode it.

00:34:51.330 --> 00:34:53.969
We will look at some pitfalls
for these sorts of models

00:34:53.969 --> 00:34:54.480
very soon.

00:35:02.330 --> 00:35:04.980
I don't have a model.

00:35:04.980 --> 00:35:06.453
What can I do?

00:35:06.453 --> 00:35:07.620
I am just getting this data.

00:35:07.620 --> 00:35:08.910
I don't have a model.

00:35:08.910 --> 00:35:11.370
Any suggestions?

00:35:11.370 --> 00:35:13.770
Can somebody explain what
they did in homework 1

00:35:13.770 --> 00:35:16.080
when they didn't have a
model, when they didn't know

00:35:16.080 --> 00:35:19.090
the probability distribution?

00:35:19.090 --> 00:35:20.750
The fifth question.

00:35:20.750 --> 00:35:22.420
I think this did--

00:35:22.420 --> 00:35:25.150
I think in the homework, we
would do like, I don't know,

00:35:25.150 --> 00:35:27.340
50 kilobytes, and we
just do blocks and look

00:35:27.340 --> 00:35:30.110
at the empirical distribution.

00:35:30.110 --> 00:35:30.610
Yep.

00:35:30.610 --> 00:35:30.880
Yep.

00:35:30.880 --> 00:35:31.380
Yep.

00:35:31.380 --> 00:35:34.750
So the answer is in question 5
in homework 1, what you did was,

00:35:34.750 --> 00:35:36.400
we were doing Huffman
coding back then,

00:35:36.400 --> 00:35:41.560
you would look at the empirical
distribution of the input data.

00:35:41.560 --> 00:35:43.960
You would just make a
model from the data and use

00:35:43.960 --> 00:35:45.185
that to compress the data.

00:35:45.185 --> 00:35:47.560
We used Huffman there because
you hadn't done arithmetic,

00:35:47.560 --> 00:35:51.190
but you could have done
arithmetic coding also.

00:35:51.190 --> 00:35:54.340
And what was the model
you used in that question?

00:35:54.340 --> 00:35:56.170
Did you assume it
was a Markov model?

00:36:01.450 --> 00:36:04.420
No, because we
used IID back then.

00:36:04.420 --> 00:36:07.600
So we just used a
simple IID model.

00:36:07.600 --> 00:36:10.180
So there are two ways
to go about this.

00:36:10.180 --> 00:36:13.630
The first way is you first
build a model from data

00:36:13.630 --> 00:36:16.710
and then encode
using that model.

00:36:16.710 --> 00:36:20.790
This is the thing
you did in homework.

00:36:20.790 --> 00:36:23.010
The other way which will
look at more in this lecture

00:36:23.010 --> 00:36:26.910
is adaptive where you build
the model from the data

00:36:26.910 --> 00:36:28.620
as you see the data.

00:36:28.620 --> 00:36:32.452
So as you see the data, you
keep on building a model.

00:36:32.452 --> 00:36:34.660
I think this is the first
time we are doing something

00:36:34.660 --> 00:36:35.200
like this.

00:36:38.080 --> 00:36:40.720
Let me show a block
diagram before we

00:36:40.720 --> 00:36:43.360
look at the pros and cons
of these two approaches.

00:36:43.360 --> 00:36:47.080
So again, past context,
you have a model.

00:36:47.080 --> 00:36:50.280
But now the model is not fixed.

00:36:50.280 --> 00:36:53.680
At every step, the model
gives you the probability

00:36:53.680 --> 00:36:54.550
for the next symbol.

00:36:54.550 --> 00:37:00.340
You encode the next symbol,
and then you update the model,

00:37:00.340 --> 00:37:03.220
and then you encode.

00:37:03.220 --> 00:37:07.000
So basically as the model
sees more and more data,

00:37:07.000 --> 00:37:08.740
it keeps on updating its state.

00:37:08.740 --> 00:37:11.268
It keeps on updating
its own model basically.

00:37:11.268 --> 00:37:12.310
The model keeps updating.

00:37:12.310 --> 00:37:14.620
It keeps training.

00:37:14.620 --> 00:37:17.800
We will do an example of this.

00:37:17.800 --> 00:37:22.630
But for now, can anybody
tell me how would you

00:37:22.630 --> 00:37:23.830
decode in this setting?

00:37:23.830 --> 00:37:27.510
Is decoding even possible
with this sort of thing?

00:37:27.510 --> 00:37:29.400
What would the
decoder need to do?

00:37:32.400 --> 00:37:33.420
This is adaptive.

00:37:33.420 --> 00:37:37.417
You are updating the model at
every step based on all the data

00:37:37.417 --> 00:37:38.500
you have seen in the past.

00:37:45.870 --> 00:37:48.270
Since the decoder is decoding
one symbol at a time,

00:37:48.270 --> 00:37:50.400
it just needs to update
its model accordingly

00:37:50.400 --> 00:37:51.690
for each symbol it sees.

00:37:51.690 --> 00:37:53.400
So in that way, it keeps it.

00:37:53.400 --> 00:37:56.980
Basically, it's the same state
in the model as the encoder did.

00:37:56.980 --> 00:37:57.480
Yep.

00:37:57.480 --> 00:37:57.780
Yep.

00:37:57.780 --> 00:37:58.280
Yep.

00:37:58.280 --> 00:38:01.710
So the answer is decoder will
decoder will decode a symbol,

00:38:01.710 --> 00:38:02.580
it will update.

00:38:02.580 --> 00:38:06.060
So we can write it down as
a sequence of operations.

00:38:06.060 --> 00:38:08.270
So you take the decoder.

00:38:08.270 --> 00:38:14.850
So it will first decode U1,
then it will update model.

00:38:14.850 --> 00:38:17.430
It will need to update the
model in exactly the same way

00:38:17.430 --> 00:38:18.360
the encoder did.

00:38:18.360 --> 00:38:23.340
At any time if the models
diverge, everything is lost.

00:38:23.340 --> 00:38:26.925
Decode U2, then you
again update model.

00:38:30.490 --> 00:38:34.150
So it basically does exactly
what the encoder does.

00:38:34.150 --> 00:38:36.720
They go in sync.

00:38:36.720 --> 00:38:38.160
And this is very, very crucial.

00:38:38.160 --> 00:38:40.680
The encoder and decoder
needs to share the same state

00:38:40.680 --> 00:38:41.460
at every step.

00:38:41.460 --> 00:38:43.230
If at any step the
arithmetic coder

00:38:43.230 --> 00:38:46.890
sees different distributions,
it will just break down.

00:38:46.890 --> 00:38:49.860
At any step if it breaks
down, then all the other steps

00:38:49.860 --> 00:38:52.290
are gone.

00:38:52.290 --> 00:38:55.110
This is fine for
simple models, but when

00:38:55.110 --> 00:38:57.300
you try to do stuff
with neural networks

00:38:57.300 --> 00:38:59.940
and so on where all
these libraries have

00:38:59.940 --> 00:39:04.110
all sorts of randomness,
things just get very bad.

00:39:04.110 --> 00:39:05.400
It's very hard.

00:39:05.400 --> 00:39:08.070
Because when you're working with
machine learning applications,

00:39:08.070 --> 00:39:11.370
for ChatGPT, it's not
important for a given input

00:39:11.370 --> 00:39:13.410
to always produce
the same output.

00:39:13.410 --> 00:39:14.850
But for a compression
model, it's

00:39:14.850 --> 00:39:17.160
very important that
given a certain input,

00:39:17.160 --> 00:39:19.950
it should always give you
the same probabilities.

00:39:19.950 --> 00:39:23.370
There can't be any GPU
level randomization.

00:39:23.370 --> 00:39:27.450
There can't be any
floating point stuff.

00:39:27.450 --> 00:39:30.343
So it's hard.

00:39:30.343 --> 00:39:32.010
People have to write
their own libraries

00:39:32.010 --> 00:39:35.820
if they want to do compression
with neural nets basically.

00:39:35.820 --> 00:39:38.680
Even if you write something,
then it works on one GPU,

00:39:38.680 --> 00:39:40.390
so you compress a
file on one GPU.

00:39:40.390 --> 00:39:44.590
You decode it on another
GPU and that breaks.

00:39:44.590 --> 00:39:46.825
So yes, very crucial.

00:39:49.340 --> 00:39:52.400
This one is also very important.

00:39:52.400 --> 00:39:55.070
During compression, you
have the entire input.

00:39:55.070 --> 00:39:56.810
But you shouldn't get too eager.

00:39:56.810 --> 00:39:59.600
Don't straight away
update the model with Ui

00:39:59.600 --> 00:40:01.400
before you encode Ui.

00:40:01.400 --> 00:40:03.020
Always think of the decoder.

00:40:03.020 --> 00:40:05.480
It's always important
to think of the decoder.

00:40:05.480 --> 00:40:07.670
Whenever each step you
think of the decoder,

00:40:07.670 --> 00:40:10.760
what does the decoder
know at this point?

00:40:10.760 --> 00:40:12.890
So let's say the
encoder does this.

00:40:16.570 --> 00:40:22.620
It first updates model with U1.

00:40:22.620 --> 00:40:26.210
And then it encodes U1.

00:40:26.210 --> 00:40:28.680
What will happen now?

00:40:28.680 --> 00:40:31.870
Can the decoder still work?

00:40:31.870 --> 00:40:33.160
I hear no.

00:40:33.160 --> 00:40:35.740
No clearly because
decoder doesn't have U1.

00:40:35.740 --> 00:40:36.970
Encoder has the entire thing.

00:40:36.970 --> 00:40:40.150
Encoder can do whatever,
but the decoder can't start,

00:40:40.150 --> 00:40:43.750
so always, this is wrong, bad.

00:40:43.750 --> 00:40:45.880
So you just need to be careful.

00:40:45.880 --> 00:40:47.590
This comes up in lossy
compression also.

00:40:47.590 --> 00:40:51.250
There are these adaptive type
schemes, just be careful.

00:40:51.250 --> 00:40:55.430
Last thing is never provide
zero probability to any symbol

00:40:55.430 --> 00:40:59.870
because if your model says that
this symbol will never occur

00:40:59.870 --> 00:41:03.320
and then it actually occurs,
then log of 1 over zero

00:41:03.320 --> 00:41:04.730
is infinity.

00:41:04.730 --> 00:41:08.410
So the arithmetic order
will just break basically.

00:41:08.410 --> 00:41:10.960
So every symbol should,
however, small it might be,

00:41:10.960 --> 00:41:13.960
but it should always have
a non-zero probability.

00:41:13.960 --> 00:41:17.380
Even if you think
that a Q after a Q

00:41:17.380 --> 00:41:19.510
will never happen in
English, provide it

00:41:19.510 --> 00:41:20.830
some small probability.

00:41:20.830 --> 00:41:23.650
Never provide it exactly
zero probability.

00:41:26.870 --> 00:41:27.860
Any questions?

00:41:33.540 --> 00:41:38.160
So just like a pros and
cons list, two-pass.

00:41:38.160 --> 00:41:40.650
So one is that we learn
model from entire data.

00:41:40.650 --> 00:41:42.090
So you look at the entire data.

00:41:42.090 --> 00:41:44.490
So maybe you get a better
model at the end of the day.

00:41:44.490 --> 00:41:46.920
And you can parallelize
the entire process more

00:41:46.920 --> 00:41:48.842
because adaptive is sequential.

00:41:48.842 --> 00:41:50.550
You're going through
the data one by one.

00:41:50.550 --> 00:41:53.460
In the two-parts, there
are ways to parallelize it.

00:41:53.460 --> 00:41:56.190
However, you need to store the
model in the compressed file

00:41:56.190 --> 00:41:58.410
so that's not ideal.

00:41:58.410 --> 00:42:00.090
You need two passes
over the data.

00:42:00.090 --> 00:42:02.760
This is often a breaking
thing in many applications

00:42:02.760 --> 00:42:04.830
where you can't
just hold the data.

00:42:04.830 --> 00:42:07.350
You have to keep compressing
the data as it comes in.

00:42:07.350 --> 00:42:09.570
You can't keep the
entire data in memory.

00:42:09.570 --> 00:42:13.310
So this is usually a problem.

00:42:13.310 --> 00:42:19.000
And if your data has statistics
that change over time,

00:42:19.000 --> 00:42:22.150
if you have a file
which start with a novel

00:42:22.150 --> 00:42:24.910
and then it suddenly turns
into a code or something,

00:42:24.910 --> 00:42:28.060
then the model you learn will
be a mixture of the two models,

00:42:28.060 --> 00:42:29.620
and it won't do as well.

00:42:29.620 --> 00:42:33.250
Whereas adaptive might
work even in this case.

00:42:33.250 --> 00:42:34.870
So adaptive, the
nice thing is you

00:42:34.870 --> 00:42:36.550
don't need to store the model.

00:42:36.550 --> 00:42:38.170
It is good for streaming.

00:42:38.170 --> 00:42:41.950
You can compress as it goes.

00:42:41.950 --> 00:42:44.830
One bad thing is that initially,
the model is very bad.

00:42:44.830 --> 00:42:45.850
You don't know anything.

00:42:45.850 --> 00:42:48.110
You don't assume
anything about the fold.

00:42:48.110 --> 00:42:50.550
So initially, you might get
slightly bad compression.

00:42:50.550 --> 00:42:52.130
But it works well in practice.

00:42:52.130 --> 00:42:57.000
So we'll look at one
example of adaptive shortly.

00:42:57.000 --> 00:42:59.880
Any questions on adaptive
arithmetic coding?

00:42:59.880 --> 00:43:06.150
Otherwise, I think we'll go to
the example in a few slides.

00:43:10.500 --> 00:43:13.980
So just wrapping this
up, this is the relation

00:43:13.980 --> 00:43:16.730
between compression
and prediction

00:43:16.730 --> 00:43:17.990
that we have been seeing.

00:43:17.990 --> 00:43:22.210
So if you have done any
machine learning class,

00:43:22.210 --> 00:43:25.540
either neural networks or
without neural networks or AI

00:43:25.540 --> 00:43:29.830
or whatever, you might have
seen this cross-entropy loss,

00:43:29.830 --> 00:43:34.200
also called log
loss for prediction.

00:43:34.200 --> 00:43:37.590
For prediction your task is you
have a bunch of classes and then

00:43:37.590 --> 00:43:40.320
you're trying to
predict which is the--

00:43:40.320 --> 00:43:43.080
you're given an
image of something

00:43:43.080 --> 00:43:45.930
and you want to tell whether
it's a cat or a dog or a cow.

00:43:45.930 --> 00:43:49.080
And most of the predictors,
what they actually give you

00:43:49.080 --> 00:43:50.010
is a probability.

00:43:50.010 --> 00:43:56.370
So they give that 90% cat,
10% dog, 10% cow, let's say.

00:43:56.370 --> 00:43:59.880
And then you evaluate the model
according to this loss function

00:43:59.880 --> 00:44:05.220
where you say that
this is a cat,

00:44:05.220 --> 00:44:14.250
and the model gives you cat
is 90%, dog is 5, cow is 5.

00:44:14.250 --> 00:44:19.080
Then the loss you get
is log 1 over 0.9,

00:44:19.080 --> 00:44:22.980
the probability of being a
cat according to the model.

00:44:22.980 --> 00:44:25.388
So this forces the model to
give a higher probability

00:44:25.388 --> 00:44:27.180
to the correct symbol
and lower probability

00:44:27.180 --> 00:44:29.880
to the not correct image--

00:44:29.880 --> 00:44:32.430
and not correct label.

00:44:32.430 --> 00:44:36.877
But this exactly matches the
thing we were just doing.

00:44:36.877 --> 00:44:38.460
So actually prediction
and compression

00:44:38.460 --> 00:44:39.810
are exactly the same problem.

00:44:43.310 --> 00:44:44.960
Not much more to
say, you can see

00:44:44.960 --> 00:44:46.220
the loss function is the same.

00:44:46.220 --> 00:44:49.940
It's literally the
same loss function.

00:44:49.940 --> 00:44:52.670
The cost and arithmetic
coding of encoding a thing

00:44:52.670 --> 00:44:55.580
is exactly the same
as the prediction loss

00:44:55.580 --> 00:45:00.290
that we always use in ML.

00:45:00.290 --> 00:45:01.910
So what this tells
you that if you

00:45:01.910 --> 00:45:04.100
have a good predictor
in the ML sense,

00:45:04.100 --> 00:45:06.630
then it's a good compressor.

00:45:06.630 --> 00:45:08.730
And compression is
all about having

00:45:08.730 --> 00:45:09.907
a good model for the data.

00:45:09.907 --> 00:45:11.490
If you have a good
model for the data,

00:45:11.490 --> 00:45:13.407
then you automatically
have a good compressor.

00:45:13.407 --> 00:45:16.930
You don't need to
do anything special.

00:45:16.930 --> 00:45:19.660
Many compressors don't
actually model the data.

00:45:19.660 --> 00:45:21.070
We'll see some.

00:45:21.070 --> 00:45:23.890
LZ77, the one we'll see, it
doesn't actually model the data.

00:45:23.890 --> 00:45:27.460
But the compressors we see today
will actually model the data.

00:45:27.460 --> 00:45:31.990
So it need not be explicit
but inherently, there

00:45:31.990 --> 00:45:34.060
is a modeling going on.

00:45:34.060 --> 00:45:37.660
And final thing,
this is out of scope.

00:45:37.660 --> 00:45:40.000
We won't go into the
details, but we are just

00:45:40.000 --> 00:45:41.140
doing arithmetic coding.

00:45:41.140 --> 00:45:44.500
But in many cases, you can
use rANS with some tricks.

00:45:44.500 --> 00:45:47.320
And people are slowly
trying to use rANS

00:45:47.320 --> 00:45:48.940
in a lot of these applications.

00:45:48.940 --> 00:45:51.955
There are some issues, but
some of them can be worked out.

00:45:56.040 --> 00:45:57.990
There is the
opposite angle also.

00:45:57.990 --> 00:46:01.870
Not only does every predictor
give you a compressor,

00:46:01.870 --> 00:46:05.560
actually every compressor
gives you a predictor.

00:46:05.560 --> 00:46:11.755
This is a bit trickier
but it is true.

00:46:14.510 --> 00:46:15.740
And here the idea is--

00:46:19.590 --> 00:46:24.890
so if you have a
compressor, encodes

00:46:24.890 --> 00:46:31.920
x1 through xn in l
bits, then the predictor

00:46:31.920 --> 00:46:36.345
that the compressor gives you is
basically given by this thing.

00:46:39.300 --> 00:46:42.390
So basically each
compressor is a predictor.

00:46:42.390 --> 00:46:45.660
Each predictor is a
compressor internally.

00:46:45.660 --> 00:46:48.420
You remember in the prediction
thing, you have log 1 by P.

00:46:48.420 --> 00:46:51.810
So it's just like you do
the formula, log 1 over P.

00:46:51.810 --> 00:46:54.330
So P is equal to
2 to the minus l.

00:46:54.330 --> 00:46:56.940
So from any compressor, you can
actually create a probability

00:46:56.940 --> 00:46:59.220
model for your data.

00:46:59.220 --> 00:47:00.840
This is slightly advanced.

00:47:00.840 --> 00:47:06.000
We will not worry too much
about it, but it is a thing.

00:47:06.000 --> 00:47:09.150
There is a paper I will tell you
about, very recent paper, that

00:47:09.150 --> 00:47:12.150
actually took gzip, which
is a very common compressor,

00:47:12.150 --> 00:47:15.490
and turned it into a predictor.

00:47:15.490 --> 00:47:20.780
It didn't do very well, but
you can do these things.

00:47:20.780 --> 00:47:23.330
So prediction models
used for compression.

00:47:23.330 --> 00:47:25.580
So now we'll look at a few
specific prediction models,

00:47:25.580 --> 00:47:32.390
do some examples, and then do
a few very fun experiments.

00:47:35.970 --> 00:47:38.760
So this is k-th order
adaptive arithmetic coding.

00:47:43.855 --> 00:47:45.230
So what you are
trying to do here

00:47:45.230 --> 00:47:48.200
is you are trying to learn a
k-th order model for your data

00:47:48.200 --> 00:47:49.595
as you go through the data.

00:47:53.830 --> 00:47:55.082
Let's just read the last line.

00:47:55.082 --> 00:47:56.290
Don't look at the rest of it.

00:47:56.290 --> 00:47:59.930
I think you will understand
from the example the rest of it.

00:47:59.930 --> 00:48:03.860
So if you have seen BANA
in the past, several times,

00:48:03.860 --> 00:48:07.130
and 90% of the times you saw
it being followed by an N,

00:48:07.130 --> 00:48:08.900
so for a banana, for example.

00:48:08.900 --> 00:48:12.440
And 10% of the time you
saw it followed by an L.

00:48:12.440 --> 00:48:13.970
That is your model basically.

00:48:13.970 --> 00:48:15.860
The next time you
see BANA, you would

00:48:15.860 --> 00:48:18.590
say that maybe it's an
N with probability 90%

00:48:18.590 --> 00:48:21.590
and L with a probability 10%.

00:48:21.590 --> 00:48:25.400
You use the past to predict
the future, as simple as that.

00:48:25.400 --> 00:48:30.620
So let's do an example,
which should make it clear.

00:48:30.620 --> 00:48:33.860
So we are doing first order
adaptive arithmetic coding.

00:48:33.860 --> 00:48:35.780
So this means you
need to actually keep

00:48:35.780 --> 00:48:38.270
second order statistics.

00:48:38.270 --> 00:48:39.590
You will understand why.

00:48:42.140 --> 00:48:45.110
So initially-- and
this is your data.

00:48:45.110 --> 00:48:47.540
You are going to encode 101011.

00:48:47.540 --> 00:48:51.350
So initially, you
assume everything is 1.

00:48:51.350 --> 00:48:53.300
You have some
frequencies or counts.

00:48:53.300 --> 00:48:54.500
So you assume that--

00:48:54.500 --> 00:48:56.660
this is to avoid the
problem we talked about

00:48:56.660 --> 00:48:58.340
before where you
don't want anything

00:48:58.340 --> 00:48:59.540
to have a 0 probability.

00:48:59.540 --> 00:49:01.640
So you assume that
you've already seen a 00.

00:49:01.640 --> 00:49:02.480
You have seen 01.

00:49:02.480 --> 00:49:03.290
You have seen a 10.

00:49:03.290 --> 00:49:04.370
You have seen a 11.

00:49:04.370 --> 00:49:09.790
You have seen all four
one time in the past.

00:49:09.790 --> 00:49:12.760
So the first symbol
you see is 1.

00:49:12.760 --> 00:49:14.110
And here you don't have a past.

00:49:14.110 --> 00:49:16.870
So what we will do is
we'll add a 0 at the front,

00:49:16.870 --> 00:49:21.780
just as a fake zero, padding.

00:49:21.780 --> 00:49:28.620
Now looking at
these counts, what

00:49:28.620 --> 00:49:31.500
is the probability that
a 0 is followed by a 1?

00:49:31.500 --> 00:49:33.300
If your past is 0,
what is the probability

00:49:33.300 --> 00:49:34.590
that the next one is a 1?

00:49:41.280 --> 00:49:45.660
How many think it's one, the
probability that a 0 is followed

00:49:45.660 --> 00:49:47.960
by a 1?

00:49:47.960 --> 00:49:51.155
How many think it's 1/3?

00:49:51.155 --> 00:49:51.655
1/2.

00:49:54.320 --> 00:49:55.640
Very few words for 1/2.

00:49:55.640 --> 00:50:01.070
1/2 is the correct answer,
so not very tricky.

00:50:01.070 --> 00:50:03.590
So basically what
we are doing this--

00:50:03.590 --> 00:50:05.900
this is the equation.

00:50:05.900 --> 00:50:07.815
The probability that
a 0 is followed by 1

00:50:07.815 --> 00:50:09.440
is you take all the
cases where you say

00:50:09.440 --> 00:50:11.840
0, comma 1 in the numerator.

00:50:11.840 --> 00:50:14.510
And you take all the cases
where you see 0, comma 0 plus 0,

00:50:14.510 --> 00:50:18.260
comma 1 in the denominator,
that sort of thing.

00:50:18.260 --> 00:50:21.290
So basically, what
this is saying is you

00:50:21.290 --> 00:50:28.080
saw 0, comma 1 n01.

00:50:28.080 --> 00:50:32.490
Let's say you saw this 99 times.

00:50:32.490 --> 00:50:37.960
And you saw 0, comma 0 one time.

00:50:37.960 --> 00:50:40.300
Then you would say that
the probability that 0 will

00:50:40.300 --> 00:50:46.330
be followed by 1 is 9 over 10.

00:50:46.330 --> 00:50:48.760
So just 9 out of
10 times, you're

00:50:48.760 --> 00:50:53.290
seeing that a 0 will be followed
by 1, as simple as that.

00:50:53.290 --> 00:50:55.330
And now you have
seen a 0, comma 1.

00:50:55.330 --> 00:50:56.620
So you update your count.

00:50:56.620 --> 00:50:58.840
You update your count
of 0, comma 1 by 1.

00:50:58.840 --> 00:51:02.802
So you do 1 plus 1 equal to 2.

00:51:02.802 --> 00:51:03.760
Are we happy with this?

00:51:03.760 --> 00:51:04.885
And then we'll do the next.

00:51:07.510 --> 00:51:09.130
Let me pause for a sec.

00:51:19.950 --> 00:51:21.870
And now we do the next symbol.

00:51:21.870 --> 00:51:24.670
So next symbol is 0.

00:51:24.670 --> 00:51:27.040
So now you need to and
the previous symbol is 1.

00:51:27.040 --> 00:51:29.380
So next symbol is 0,
previous symbol is 1.

00:51:29.380 --> 00:51:32.650
So you do probability of 0
given 1 based on this model.

00:51:32.650 --> 00:51:34.270
Just follow the math.

00:51:34.270 --> 00:51:35.440
Again, you get one 1/2.

00:51:35.440 --> 00:51:37.720
Again, you update the model.

00:51:37.720 --> 00:51:40.530
Let me do the next one,
which is more interesting.

00:51:40.530 --> 00:51:43.880
So now you have
your previous symbol

00:51:43.880 --> 00:51:46.730
is a 0 and next symbol is a 1.

00:51:46.730 --> 00:51:48.500
But remember that
due to the padding,

00:51:48.500 --> 00:51:50.890
you have already seen 0, 1.

00:51:50.890 --> 00:51:53.560
So now your model should
lean towards, oh, maybe

00:51:53.560 --> 00:51:57.010
a 0 is more often followed by 1
and less often followed by a 0.

00:51:57.010 --> 00:51:58.660
And yeah, that's what it does.

00:51:58.660 --> 00:52:02.320
It gives you a 2/3 probability
instead of a 1/2 probability

00:52:02.320 --> 00:52:04.480
which it gave you last time.

00:52:04.480 --> 00:52:06.490
So as you see more
and more data,

00:52:06.490 --> 00:52:09.040
it will learn the
statistics of your data

00:52:09.040 --> 00:52:11.830
and start giving you
predictions which

00:52:11.830 --> 00:52:13.330
are consistent with your data.

00:52:13.330 --> 00:52:16.360
And at every step, you
keep updating your model.

00:52:16.360 --> 00:52:19.210
I think you will have a
chance to play with this,

00:52:19.210 --> 00:52:22.280
actually make your own model in
one of the homework questions.

00:52:22.280 --> 00:52:23.485
So that should be exciting.

00:52:26.763 --> 00:52:28.180
So the idea is
that over time, you

00:52:28.180 --> 00:52:30.700
learn the empirical
distribution of the data

00:52:30.700 --> 00:52:33.310
as you see the
more and more data.

00:52:33.310 --> 00:52:35.650
And initially you start off
with a uniform distribution

00:52:35.650 --> 00:52:37.390
in the example we did.

00:52:37.390 --> 00:52:39.520
But if you know
something about the data,

00:52:39.520 --> 00:52:43.840
you can use that to
initialize your counts.

00:52:43.840 --> 00:52:46.840
Make sure encoder and
decoder both know, and also

00:52:46.840 --> 00:52:49.750
make sure nothing
is 0 basically.

00:52:49.750 --> 00:52:52.930
Never make anything 0 because
if you make anything zero, then

00:52:52.930 --> 00:52:55.390
everything breaks.

00:52:55.390 --> 00:52:59.470
And one final thing for that
question from homework 1

00:52:59.470 --> 00:53:03.430
that you all did, you could have
used this with k equal to 0.

00:53:03.430 --> 00:53:06.310
You could have-- you
could set k equal to 0.

00:53:06.310 --> 00:53:12.580
So what you would do is, let's
say you have seen 1011000111

00:53:12.580 --> 00:53:14.380
and the next symbol
is a 0, which

00:53:14.380 --> 00:53:16.270
is what you want to encode.

00:53:16.270 --> 00:53:19.270
So in the past, you
have seen 1, 2, 3, 4, 5,

00:53:19.270 --> 00:53:23.700
6 so six 1's and four 0's.

00:53:23.700 --> 00:53:28.890
So you could just make a
model where this is 4 by 10.

00:53:28.890 --> 00:53:36.580
And then the next symbol, let's
say that's also 0, can anybody

00:53:36.580 --> 00:53:38.500
tell me what will be the
predicted probability

00:53:38.500 --> 00:53:41.980
of the next symbol if this guy
had a probability of 4 over 10?

00:53:46.850 --> 00:53:48.410
5 over 11, I hear.

00:53:48.410 --> 00:53:52.720
Yes, I think, because the last
symbol will increase the count

00:53:52.720 --> 00:53:54.850
of 0's and increase
the count of 1's.

00:53:54.850 --> 00:53:59.570
So just the counts,
nothing special.

00:53:59.570 --> 00:54:04.100
So you can implement it.

00:54:04.100 --> 00:54:07.290
We implemented it.

00:54:07.290 --> 00:54:14.370
We took The Hound of
Baskerville, and we ran it.

00:54:14.370 --> 00:54:17.430
So 0th order is basically
assuming an IID model.

00:54:17.430 --> 00:54:19.440
First order assumes
a first order model,

00:54:19.440 --> 00:54:20.820
second order, third order.

00:54:20.820 --> 00:54:22.860
We also ran two
standard compressors

00:54:22.860 --> 00:54:27.090
as benchmarks, gzip and bzip2.

00:54:27.090 --> 00:54:30.000
So somebody tell me why
does it improve when you

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:31.560
go from zero to second order?

00:54:47.390 --> 00:54:49.520
Let's say your
input was actually.

00:54:49.520 --> 00:54:50.690
There was no dependence.

00:54:50.690 --> 00:54:54.280
Do you expect it to improve
if you use a larger context?

00:54:54.280 --> 00:54:55.300
No, right?

00:54:55.300 --> 00:54:59.000
So clearly, there
is some relation.

00:54:59.000 --> 00:55:00.260
And you know that English has.

00:55:00.260 --> 00:55:01.790
English, if the
past two symbols,

00:55:01.790 --> 00:55:04.220
you can predict the
next symbol better.

00:55:04.220 --> 00:55:07.280
So therefore, it will improve
as you increase the order.

00:55:07.280 --> 00:55:09.395
Now can somebody tell
me why did it not

00:55:09.395 --> 00:55:10.520
get better for third order.

00:55:10.520 --> 00:55:13.740
Why did it actually get worse?

00:55:13.740 --> 00:55:16.020
Recall that the file
has a certain size.

00:55:16.020 --> 00:55:17.460
It's a few 100 kilobytes.

00:55:17.460 --> 00:55:19.650
It's not infinite.

00:55:25.960 --> 00:55:29.230
In a smallish
file, do you expect

00:55:29.230 --> 00:55:34.522
to see a sequence A,
B, C, D or something?

00:55:34.522 --> 00:55:36.230
Do you expect to see
a sequence of length

00:55:36.230 --> 00:55:39.680
four many, many times
in a very short file?

00:55:39.680 --> 00:55:42.200
If you have a small
amount of data,

00:55:42.200 --> 00:55:43.700
you won't even get any counts.

00:55:43.700 --> 00:55:44.960
That is the issue.

00:55:44.960 --> 00:55:48.260
You start with your
initial uniform model

00:55:48.260 --> 00:55:50.420
and you never get
enough counts to make

00:55:50.420 --> 00:55:52.430
that model into something
useful because you

00:55:52.430 --> 00:55:56.660
have such a small-- because if
you think about it, the count

00:55:56.660 --> 00:56:06.540
array basically, so for
first order, you need pairs.

00:56:06.540 --> 00:56:10.940
You need Xi, comma Xi plus 1.

00:56:10.940 --> 00:56:15.980
And then for second order, you
need Xi, Xi plus 1, Xi plus 2.

00:56:15.980 --> 00:56:19.290
You need three at
a time, and so on.

00:56:19.290 --> 00:56:22.700
So this grows exponentially
in the order basically.

00:56:22.700 --> 00:56:26.120
So if your alphabet
size is X and your--

00:56:26.120 --> 00:56:28.520
so this is your order.

00:56:28.520 --> 00:56:32.520
Then this is the number of
counts you need to maintain.

00:56:32.520 --> 00:56:34.740
So if you have a very
small amount of data,

00:56:34.740 --> 00:56:36.480
your counts will be very sparse.

00:56:36.480 --> 00:56:37.680
Most of them will be 0.

00:56:37.680 --> 00:56:40.545
You won't see most
of the patterns.

00:56:40.545 --> 00:56:42.420
And since you don't see
most of the patterns,

00:56:42.420 --> 00:56:47.010
you get a very poor predictor,
and it doesn't do well.

00:56:47.010 --> 00:56:49.410
If you don't have a lot of
data, then higher order models

00:56:49.410 --> 00:56:53.740
start breaking because we
have a very silly model.

00:56:53.740 --> 00:56:56.700
It just looks at the past.

00:56:56.700 --> 00:57:06.880
And so for example, just as an
example, let's say you see THIN

00:57:06.880 --> 00:57:09.520
and then you're trying to
predict the next symbol.

00:57:09.520 --> 00:57:12.220
But if you have never
seen THIN in the past,

00:57:12.220 --> 00:57:14.050
you have no model for it.

00:57:14.050 --> 00:57:15.847
So it won't do anything useful.

00:57:15.847 --> 00:57:17.680
So unless you have a
large enough text where

00:57:17.680 --> 00:57:21.730
you see that thing many, many
times, it won't learn anything.

00:57:21.730 --> 00:57:25.350
So that's why it got worse here.

00:57:25.350 --> 00:57:28.230
And we see that gzip and bzip2
actually beat these models.

00:57:28.230 --> 00:57:32.620
And we'll talk about
them in next lecture.

00:57:32.620 --> 00:57:34.630
So we talked already
about why is order

00:57:34.630 --> 00:57:36.850
3 to inverse than order 2.

00:57:36.850 --> 00:57:38.650
So there are limitations.

00:57:38.650 --> 00:57:40.090
k-th order becomes slow.

00:57:40.090 --> 00:57:42.820
The memory grows
exponentially in k.

00:57:42.820 --> 00:57:45.160
The counts become very
sparse for large k.

00:57:45.160 --> 00:57:47.860
As you increase k,
the count is just--

00:57:47.860 --> 00:57:52.490
you don't have many counts and
it leads to worse performance.

00:57:52.490 --> 00:57:56.720
And this is an
interesting point where--

00:58:01.210 --> 00:58:03.260
just an example.

00:58:03.260 --> 00:58:06.410
Let's say you have
"thi" and capital THI.

00:58:06.410 --> 00:58:08.990
If you had a smart model,
it would know that OK

00:58:08.990 --> 00:58:11.840
these two just the
capitalization is different,

00:58:11.840 --> 00:58:13.100
but there is some relation.

00:58:13.100 --> 00:58:19.010
If you have seen THIS many,
many times even for uppercase T,

00:58:19.010 --> 00:58:22.580
it should predict that
this is-- maybe it's a S.

00:58:22.580 --> 00:58:25.610
But if you have that silly
adaptive arithmetic coder

00:58:25.610 --> 00:58:27.890
k-th order model,
that doesn't know.

00:58:27.890 --> 00:58:29.390
Everything looks
different to it.

00:58:29.390 --> 00:58:31.310
It has no understanding
of English.

00:58:31.310 --> 00:58:33.080
It has no understanding
of anything.

00:58:33.080 --> 00:58:37.100
And it just treats any two
contexts, any two past sequence

00:58:37.100 --> 00:58:42.890
as just distinct, just those two
separate counts for all of them.

00:58:42.890 --> 00:58:44.900
So it doesn't do as well.

00:58:44.900 --> 00:58:46.145
We can improve them.

00:58:48.780 --> 00:58:49.950
I said a lot of bad things.

00:58:49.950 --> 00:58:51.390
It's not the end--

00:58:51.390 --> 00:58:52.440
people use this.

00:58:52.440 --> 00:58:55.110
They are used in certain
specific scenarios,

00:58:55.110 --> 00:58:58.380
but they're not the best models.

00:58:58.380 --> 00:59:00.945
But they are simple models,
and you can try them.

00:59:04.470 --> 00:59:05.850
Now did another thing.

00:59:05.850 --> 00:59:07.740
I said I did the adaptive thing.

00:59:07.740 --> 00:59:11.040
But if I did a
two-pass thing, I can

00:59:11.040 --> 00:59:14.550
compute the conditional
entropy from the data.

00:59:14.550 --> 00:59:17.760
And now I see, oh, for 0th
order, they are very close.

00:59:17.760 --> 00:59:19.920
For first order,
there is a small gap.

00:59:19.920 --> 00:59:22.440
For third order, the
empirical conditional entropy

00:59:22.440 --> 00:59:23.610
keeps improving.

00:59:29.060 --> 00:59:31.400
So if I did a
two-pass approach, it

00:59:31.400 --> 00:59:33.560
seems like I could do a
really, really good job.

00:59:39.625 --> 00:59:41.750
Again, the Huffman case,
we did a two pass approach

00:59:41.750 --> 00:59:42.417
if you remember.

00:59:44.835 --> 00:59:45.960
Would do you do a good job?

00:59:45.960 --> 00:59:46.710
What do you think?

00:59:53.850 --> 00:59:57.670
So if you are doing
a third order model,

00:59:57.670 --> 01:00:03.860
let's say in your entire
text you see "thi" only once

01:00:03.860 --> 01:00:08.000
and it was followed by, S
let's say, in your entire text.

01:00:08.000 --> 01:00:10.850
So how many bits do
you spend to encode S?

01:00:10.850 --> 01:00:14.120
If you've seen it only
once, and the one time

01:00:14.120 --> 01:00:16.880
it was followed by
S. You basically--

01:00:16.880 --> 01:00:19.520
can somebody tell me.

01:00:19.520 --> 01:00:20.270
None.

01:00:20.270 --> 01:00:22.540
You spend 0 bits.

01:00:22.540 --> 01:00:25.840
You spend 0 bits to encode the
next symbol because you only

01:00:25.840 --> 01:00:26.380
saw it once.

01:00:26.380 --> 01:00:30.400
So the probability
distribution is deterministic.

01:00:30.400 --> 01:00:31.960
But how does the decoder know?

01:00:37.490 --> 01:00:37.990
Yes.

01:00:37.990 --> 01:00:38.230
Yes.

01:00:38.230 --> 01:00:38.470
Yes.

01:00:38.470 --> 01:00:38.970
Yes.

01:00:38.970 --> 01:00:44.200
Basically, what you are
doing is the model basically

01:00:44.200 --> 01:00:46.810
now starts storing the data.

01:00:46.810 --> 01:00:54.860
As you increase k, what happens
is think of the extreme,

01:00:54.860 --> 01:00:56.060
think of the very extreme.

01:00:56.060 --> 01:00:58.820
Let's say you have a text of
length 1,000 and if you take

01:00:58.820 --> 01:01:03.350
a 1,000th order model, then
there is only 1,000 line

01:01:03.350 --> 01:01:06.050
sequence in the entire input.

01:01:06.050 --> 01:01:07.790
It has probability 1.

01:01:07.790 --> 01:01:09.230
You encode it in 0 bits.

01:01:09.230 --> 01:01:11.390
You don't need any
bits to encode it.

01:01:11.390 --> 01:01:16.230
So this is just a thought
experiment where you take--

01:01:16.230 --> 01:01:26.910
so let's say your input is
1,000 bits and 1,000 symbols

01:01:26.910 --> 01:01:32.330
and you take 1,000 order model.

01:01:32.330 --> 01:01:36.410
So the probability
of the input is 1.

01:01:36.410 --> 01:01:47.420
Probability of
everything else is 0.

01:01:47.420 --> 01:01:49.850
You just store your
entire data in your model

01:01:49.850 --> 01:01:52.460
and you say, oh, I
encoded it in 0 bits

01:01:52.460 --> 01:01:58.160
because my model is perfect at
predicting my particular data.

01:01:58.160 --> 01:02:01.350
You take a Sherlock
Holmes novel,

01:02:01.350 --> 01:02:05.400
your model contains the
entire Sherlock Holmes novel.

01:02:05.400 --> 01:02:07.427
And you encode it in 0 bits.

01:02:07.427 --> 01:02:08.760
You don't need to send anything.

01:02:08.760 --> 01:02:11.130
The decoder knows it's
a Sherlock Holmes novel.

01:02:11.130 --> 01:02:13.620
So this is silly.

01:02:13.620 --> 01:02:15.210
This is not how we should do it.

01:02:17.940 --> 01:02:20.490
So as the order increases,
basically knowing

01:02:20.490 --> 01:02:23.280
the empirical
distribution basically

01:02:23.280 --> 01:02:25.530
becomes storing the
data in the model.

01:02:25.530 --> 01:02:27.660
So you're not doing anything
smart at that point.

01:02:27.660 --> 01:02:30.660
You're just storing
your data in the model.

01:02:30.660 --> 01:02:38.020
But recall that the model size
is exponential in the order.

01:02:38.020 --> 01:02:41.920
So as the order becomes bigger,
the model overwhelms everything.

01:02:41.920 --> 01:02:43.360
And therefore, you
need to account

01:02:43.360 --> 01:02:46.040
for the cost of
storing the model.

01:02:46.040 --> 01:02:48.560
And this is another reason
where adaptive models are often

01:02:48.560 --> 01:02:54.040
preferred because you don't
need two passes and so on.

01:02:54.040 --> 01:02:55.060
There is this principle.

01:02:55.060 --> 01:02:57.490
We won't talk too
much about it, but I

01:02:57.490 --> 01:02:59.500
think this will convey
what I was trying to say.

01:02:59.500 --> 01:03:01.660
As you make your model
more and more complex,

01:03:01.660 --> 01:03:03.910
your compressed size will
reduce because your model

01:03:03.910 --> 01:03:06.520
will at some point start
learning your data.

01:03:06.520 --> 01:03:09.700
But the model size will
increase exponentially.

01:03:09.700 --> 01:03:12.160
And so the total size
is what you care about,

01:03:12.160 --> 01:03:14.950
the size of the model
plus the size of the data.

01:03:14.950 --> 01:03:17.200
And there is some point
where it's optimal.

01:03:17.200 --> 01:03:18.640
Like in this figure, it's here.

01:03:18.640 --> 01:03:21.520
At some intermediate
model complexity,

01:03:21.520 --> 01:03:26.320
you will get the
best overall result.

01:03:26.320 --> 01:03:29.080
This principle is called
minimum description length

01:03:29.080 --> 01:03:31.420
by Rissanen, who is
one of the people who

01:03:31.420 --> 01:03:34.150
did a lot of interesting
theory in compression.

01:03:34.150 --> 01:03:38.230
And he's one of the creators
of arithmetic coding actually,

01:03:38.230 --> 01:03:43.380
so good work.

01:03:43.380 --> 01:03:47.180
any questions so far?

01:03:47.180 --> 01:03:48.680
I don't think we'll
get to LZ today,

01:03:48.680 --> 01:03:50.240
but we'll do some fun things.

01:03:53.610 --> 01:03:56.820
Any questions?

01:03:56.820 --> 01:04:00.290
So we saw adaptive
k-th order modeling.

01:04:00.290 --> 01:04:02.570
We saw what happens
if you do a two-pass

01:04:02.570 --> 01:04:04.775
and you keep
increasing the order.

01:04:04.775 --> 01:04:07.150
And we thought that the k-th
order thing has some issues.

01:04:07.150 --> 01:04:08.380
The counts becomes sparse.

01:04:08.380 --> 01:04:09.610
It gets harder.

01:04:09.610 --> 01:04:12.670
So this is something we won't
go into too much detail,

01:04:12.670 --> 01:04:15.040
but it's a great
project idea basically

01:04:15.040 --> 01:04:16.600
if you want to do any of these.

01:04:16.600 --> 01:04:20.950
So the k-th order adaptive is
implemented in the library.

01:04:20.950 --> 01:04:23.680
Solving the sparse
count problem,

01:04:23.680 --> 01:04:27.490
there are a couple of ways where
you can actually partially solve

01:04:27.490 --> 01:04:29.170
that issue.

01:04:29.170 --> 01:04:31.870
There is something called
PPM, something called CTW.

01:04:31.870 --> 01:04:35.800
And what they do is that
maybe I have not seen TH

01:04:35.800 --> 01:04:37.960
before but maybe
I've seen HI before

01:04:37.960 --> 01:04:39.820
or maybe I've seen
just I before,

01:04:39.820 --> 01:04:41.840
so you mix a bunch of things.

01:04:41.840 --> 01:04:46.627
And you try to
compensate for the fact

01:04:46.627 --> 01:04:48.460
that maybe you have not
seen longer context,

01:04:48.460 --> 01:04:50.252
but you have at least
seen shorter context.

01:04:50.252 --> 01:04:51.940
So at least use those.

01:04:51.940 --> 01:04:56.660
Don't throw away information
that you already got.

01:04:56.660 --> 01:05:00.710
Again I'm not going
into details sadly.

01:05:00.710 --> 01:05:04.160
Then there are some
advanced prediction models.

01:05:04.160 --> 01:05:04.940
I have links here.

01:05:04.940 --> 01:05:07.910
I will talk over
a couple of them.

01:05:07.910 --> 01:05:09.470
And really this book is--

01:05:09.470 --> 01:05:12.020
if you want to really understand
these and implement these,

01:05:12.020 --> 01:05:16.880
this is an online textbook
by Matt Mahoney, who

01:05:16.880 --> 01:05:20.660
has done a lot of interesting
work on heavy duty compressors

01:05:20.660 --> 01:05:22.820
in particular.

01:05:22.820 --> 01:05:25.430
So all of these are
typically the most powerful

01:05:25.430 --> 01:05:30.690
compressors around but very slow
for many practical applications.

01:05:30.690 --> 01:05:34.290
So just very quickly two
of these advanced models.

01:05:34.290 --> 01:05:36.810
Some of them are neural net
based where what they do is--

01:05:41.220 --> 01:05:45.190
yeah, so it basically you
have a given the past symbols,

01:05:45.190 --> 01:05:46.690
you have a neural
network predictor,

01:05:46.690 --> 01:05:51.770
and then that goes
into arithmetic encoder

01:05:51.770 --> 01:05:53.720
and similarly for the decoder.

01:05:53.720 --> 01:05:55.482
So we just changed
our adaptive thing

01:05:55.482 --> 01:05:57.440
to a neural network
predictor, which will again

01:05:57.440 --> 01:05:58.550
give you the probabilities.

01:05:58.550 --> 01:06:00.020
And you can do a
two-pass approach,

01:06:00.020 --> 01:06:01.370
or you can do it an
adaptive approach.

01:06:01.370 --> 01:06:03.260
So you can keep updating
your neural net.

01:06:03.260 --> 01:06:04.850
Just like you had
your count thing,

01:06:04.850 --> 01:06:07.392
you can do a neural net thing
basically is what we're saying.

01:06:10.070 --> 01:06:14.030
For CMIX, the other
one I mentioned,

01:06:14.030 --> 01:06:16.130
this is very advanced
ideas where what they do

01:06:16.130 --> 01:06:18.980
is they have thousands
of different models.

01:06:18.980 --> 01:06:20.990
You predict based
on the last bit.

01:06:20.990 --> 01:06:24.380
You predict the last two bits,
last three bits, previous word,

01:06:24.380 --> 01:06:27.410
first letter of previous word,
last letter of previous word,

01:06:27.410 --> 01:06:29.570
whether this word
is the first letter,

01:06:29.570 --> 01:06:31.700
first word in the sentence
or the second word,

01:06:31.700 --> 01:06:34.460
just a bunch of
different contexts.

01:06:34.460 --> 01:06:37.250
And then they have a neural
net that mixes all those

01:06:37.250 --> 01:06:40.850
into a single probability.

01:06:40.850 --> 01:06:42.320
There is a talk--

01:06:42.320 --> 01:06:45.050
I know you won't appreciate
from what I just said,

01:06:45.050 --> 01:06:47.570
but there is a talk from
last year's IT forum

01:06:47.570 --> 01:06:52.250
where the author of
this CMIX came to talk.

01:06:52.250 --> 01:06:56.540
And this is a slide
that Pulkit created

01:06:56.540 --> 01:07:00.080
at some point, which just shows
over time how has compression

01:07:00.080 --> 01:07:01.700
for text improved.

01:07:01.700 --> 01:07:07.070
And this is gzip, bzip2, 7zip.

01:07:07.070 --> 01:07:11.660
And here we show if you assume
that the characters are uniform,

01:07:11.660 --> 01:07:12.590
it's very bad.

01:07:12.590 --> 01:07:14.810
Bits per character
is more than four.

01:07:14.810 --> 01:07:21.050
If you assume word level some
modeling, then it gets lower.

01:07:21.050 --> 01:07:23.780
And last time, we saw
some estimate by Shannon

01:07:23.780 --> 01:07:25.250
where he did some
human experiment

01:07:25.250 --> 01:07:27.410
to try to estimate the
entropy rate of English.

01:07:27.410 --> 01:07:30.590
And we have, as you see
on the far right side,

01:07:30.590 --> 01:07:32.390
we have actually
beaten that now.

01:07:32.390 --> 01:07:34.640
With the CMIX and
NNCP models, we

01:07:34.640 --> 01:07:37.370
are doing better than Shannon's
estimate of the entropy rate.

01:07:37.370 --> 01:07:41.120
So I've really come very
far in compressing text.

01:07:41.120 --> 01:07:43.850
Even though text in many
cases is not something--

01:07:43.850 --> 01:07:45.710
there isn't a lot of text.

01:07:45.710 --> 01:07:50.150
All the text created by humans
is a few terabytes or something.

01:07:50.150 --> 01:07:52.640
It's far from the
biggest data source,

01:07:52.640 --> 01:07:55.580
but I think we are just curious
to compress text as well

01:07:55.580 --> 01:07:56.090
as possible.

01:07:58.640 --> 01:07:59.710
Any questions?

01:08:04.430 --> 01:08:06.890
So if you didn't
listen to anything,

01:08:06.890 --> 01:08:10.680
please listen to this because
I'm very excited about this.

01:08:10.680 --> 01:08:12.900
Now remember that
good prediction

01:08:12.900 --> 01:08:14.790
equal to good compression.

01:08:14.790 --> 01:08:18.330
So today the most powerful
predictors are these LLM models.

01:08:18.330 --> 01:08:19.740
So this is new material.

01:08:19.740 --> 01:08:22.770
We didn't have this last year.

01:08:22.770 --> 01:08:25.080
So now forget about
that MDL setting

01:08:25.080 --> 01:08:27.990
that we have been working
with where we said

01:08:27.990 --> 01:08:29.370
that the model is very big.

01:08:29.370 --> 01:08:29.939
That's bad.

01:08:29.939 --> 01:08:30.450
No.

01:08:30.450 --> 01:08:32.460
Now we don't worry
about storing the model.

01:08:32.460 --> 01:08:36.750
Just get the best compression,
bring your biggest model.

01:08:36.750 --> 01:08:40.689
So throw your most powerful
predictor at a problem.

01:08:40.689 --> 01:08:44.930
Gigabytes, hundreds of
gigabytes of model, that's fine.

01:08:44.930 --> 01:08:46.702
So in a normal
setting like this,

01:08:46.702 --> 01:08:49.160
this is not useful because
where would you store the model.

01:08:49.160 --> 01:08:51.380
And obviously if you store
that model with the file,

01:08:51.380 --> 01:08:53.479
that doesn't make sense.

01:08:53.479 --> 01:08:56.630
Can anyone think of
any domain or any sort

01:08:56.630 --> 01:08:59.750
of setting where it's
fine to have a very

01:08:59.750 --> 01:09:01.729
big model as a predictor?

01:09:14.810 --> 01:09:17.029
So what is the issue with
having a very big model

01:09:17.029 --> 01:09:17.930
as the predictor?

01:09:17.930 --> 01:09:19.460
We said we need
to store the model

01:09:19.460 --> 01:09:22.370
as part of the compressed file.

01:09:22.370 --> 01:09:25.330
But do we?

01:09:25.330 --> 01:09:26.319
Maybe we don't.

01:09:26.319 --> 01:09:28.240
Maybe there is a
world where everybody

01:09:28.240 --> 01:09:34.300
has GPT-4 on their mobile or
on their laptop or whatever.

01:09:34.300 --> 01:09:36.760
And then everybody assumes
that everybody else has

01:09:36.760 --> 01:09:38.410
that huge model
on their machine,

01:09:38.410 --> 01:09:40.240
and everybody uses
that single model

01:09:40.240 --> 01:09:44.229
to compress things and
send it to each other.

01:09:44.229 --> 01:09:47.260
So there are settings.

01:09:47.260 --> 01:09:50.229
So if you have a large amount of
data of the same type basically

01:09:50.229 --> 01:09:53.710
and you can afford to
deploy the model everywhere,

01:09:53.710 --> 01:09:55.630
which might become a reality.

01:09:55.630 --> 01:09:57.495
There are two other
reasons to do this.

01:09:57.495 --> 01:09:58.870
One is that we
want to understand

01:09:58.870 --> 01:10:00.730
the limits of compression.

01:10:00.730 --> 01:10:03.880
How good can your text
prediction become?

01:10:03.880 --> 01:10:07.160
And other is just to have
fun in a compression class.

01:10:07.160 --> 01:10:09.250
So for me, the
third reason really.

01:10:12.270 --> 01:10:14.070
So basic model is simple.

01:10:17.100 --> 01:10:18.780
Predictor gives you compressor.

01:10:18.780 --> 01:10:22.320
Log loss, so basically
if you think of LLMs,

01:10:22.320 --> 01:10:24.210
LLMs are trained as predictors.

01:10:24.210 --> 01:10:27.240
And their loss function is the
cross-entropy loss or 2 power

01:10:27.240 --> 01:10:29.190
the cross-entropy loss.

01:10:29.190 --> 01:10:31.680
So in effect, they are
becoming compressors.

01:10:31.680 --> 01:10:33.360
They are actually compressors.

01:10:33.360 --> 01:10:38.100
And I took this recent
work by the same person

01:10:38.100 --> 01:10:42.330
who made an NNCP, who made
this text compression using

01:10:42.330 --> 01:10:45.510
large language models where
you can download any model

01:10:45.510 --> 01:10:48.510
and then use that to
compress your favorite text.

01:10:48.510 --> 01:10:51.780
And I did like a
couple of things.

01:10:51.780 --> 01:10:55.390
So I took a 2023 novel.

01:10:55.390 --> 01:10:58.330
And I took second order
arithmetic coding.

01:10:58.330 --> 01:10:59.860
I took bzip2.

01:10:59.860 --> 01:11:01.840
And I took two models.

01:11:01.840 --> 01:11:03.730
And I took different
context lengths.

01:11:03.730 --> 01:11:06.640
So context length is how much
of the past number of tokens

01:11:06.640 --> 01:11:09.280
you see.

01:11:09.280 --> 01:11:12.330
So you see that if your contact
length is not very large.

01:11:12.330 --> 01:11:14.070
If you just look at
the last 16 tokens,

01:11:14.070 --> 01:11:17.490
it does better than bzip2
but not very impressive.

01:11:17.490 --> 01:11:21.450
And the medium size model, which
is in darker shade of blue,

01:11:21.450 --> 01:11:24.480
does slightly better
than the small model.

01:11:24.480 --> 01:11:28.650
But it really starts to shine
when you take a long context.

01:11:28.650 --> 01:11:30.960
That's the thing with these
large language models.

01:11:30.960 --> 01:11:33.347
They can look at a
very big past history

01:11:33.347 --> 01:11:35.430
and make a very good
prediction for the next token

01:11:35.430 --> 01:11:36.150
based on that.

01:11:36.150 --> 01:11:38.130
We saw it in the earlier
part of the class

01:11:38.130 --> 01:11:40.350
if you remember where the
more context you give,

01:11:40.350 --> 01:11:42.090
the better the
prediction becomes.

01:11:42.090 --> 01:11:44.670
So once you give it like
a long enough context,

01:11:44.670 --> 01:11:46.320
it starts getting
to the point where

01:11:46.320 --> 01:11:49.890
it's below the Shannon bound,
very close to CMIX and NNCP.

01:11:49.890 --> 01:11:51.150
So it basically the optimal.

01:11:51.150 --> 01:11:52.442
And this is a very small model.

01:11:52.442 --> 01:11:55.200
It's a 430 million
parameter model

01:11:55.200 --> 01:12:00.660
whereas the GPTs are
tens of billions or more.

01:12:00.660 --> 01:12:01.950
Any question on this?

01:12:01.950 --> 01:12:03.630
You generally
understand the idea.

01:12:03.630 --> 01:12:05.593
Bigger model, better
compression, or I

01:12:05.593 --> 01:12:07.260
guess, better model,
better compression.

01:12:07.260 --> 01:12:09.510
More context,
better compression,

01:12:09.510 --> 01:12:11.265
not counting the model size.

01:12:15.620 --> 01:12:17.000
Now it did this.

01:12:17.000 --> 01:12:21.890
Instead of having this English
novel released in 2023,

01:12:21.890 --> 01:12:24.530
I took a Pali is an
