01:12:45.650 --> 01:12:47.540
In these languages
we're trying to--

01:12:50.380 --> 01:12:52.292
they're like a universal model.

01:12:52.292 --> 01:12:54.500
So they're not really trained
for this specific thing

01:12:54.500 --> 01:12:58.460
where using this, we
will have the model based

01:12:58.460 --> 01:13:00.970
on the specific distribution.

01:13:00.970 --> 01:13:01.470
Yeah.

01:13:01.470 --> 01:13:01.740
Yeah.

01:13:01.740 --> 01:13:02.240
Yeah.

01:13:02.240 --> 01:13:04.800
So the idea is that these LLM
models oftentimes, the training

01:13:04.800 --> 01:13:07.540
data set is English, especially
for these smaller models,

01:13:07.540 --> 01:13:13.160
the training data set is
limited to a certain corpus.

01:13:13.160 --> 01:13:16.137
And didn't try GPT-4
because I don't have it.

01:13:16.137 --> 01:13:17.970
It would just take too
long, I think, to do.

01:13:17.970 --> 01:13:20.760
But maybe that will do better.

01:13:20.760 --> 01:13:23.853
But yes, so gzip, bzip2,
second order arithmetic coding,

01:13:23.853 --> 01:13:25.770
they don't make any
assumption about the data.

01:13:25.770 --> 01:13:27.720
They will work for
any type of data.

01:13:27.720 --> 01:13:31.680
So that is the beauty there
that they are in a sense gzip

01:13:31.680 --> 01:13:33.870
and bzip2 are
universal in a sense.

01:13:33.870 --> 01:13:35.580
They don't care about your data.

01:13:35.580 --> 01:13:38.010
Just for any type
of data you give,

01:13:38.010 --> 01:13:39.660
if it's a big enough
sample of data,

01:13:39.660 --> 01:13:42.660
they will learn the distribution
from the data and do a good job.

01:13:42.660 --> 01:13:44.940
These LLM models, they
assume that the data

01:13:44.940 --> 01:13:46.650
is of a certain distribution.

01:13:46.650 --> 01:13:49.725
And if your data is not from
that, then they do worse.

01:13:52.720 --> 01:13:55.660
Then Kedar did some
experiments where

01:13:55.660 --> 01:13:59.740
he took an even bigger model,
