01:15:25.630 --> 01:15:26.290
Anybody?

01:15:26.290 --> 01:15:29.430
Any guesses?

01:15:29.430 --> 01:15:31.170
They used old books.

01:15:31.170 --> 01:15:33.000
Yeah, yeah, they used old books.

01:15:33.000 --> 01:15:35.190
Among other things, they
looked at Wikipedia.

01:15:35.190 --> 01:15:39.390
So Llama was trained on a bunch
of things, including, I assume,

01:15:39.390 --> 01:15:42.330
the Sherlock Holmes thing was
part of the training data set.

01:15:42.330 --> 01:15:45.580
And therefore, I guess
it memorized some of it.

01:15:45.580 --> 01:15:47.550
And it's just doing
an exceptional job

01:15:47.550 --> 01:15:49.740
at compressing the
Sherlock Holmes novel.

01:15:49.740 --> 01:15:53.015
So one needs to be
careful when evaluating.

01:15:53.015 --> 01:15:54.390
That's why all of
the times we're

01:15:54.390 --> 01:15:56.700
using this 2023 thing,
which we assumed

01:15:56.700 --> 01:15:58.995
was not part of the very
recently released things.

01:16:02.580 --> 01:16:09.020
So last slide, so LLM-based
compression remarkable results

01:16:09.020 --> 01:16:11.120
can be--

01:16:11.120 --> 01:16:13.975
it's really we are
pushing it to the limit.

01:16:13.975 --> 01:16:15.350
So for a compression
perspective,

01:16:15.350 --> 01:16:17.690
it's just useful to know
even if these are not going

01:16:17.690 --> 01:16:21.595
to be the most practical ones.

01:16:21.595 --> 01:16:23.720
We need to be careful about
a couple things we saw.

01:16:23.720 --> 01:16:26.630
One is that the model and the
data, if they are not aligned,

01:16:26.630 --> 01:16:28.790
then you get not
so good results.

01:16:28.790 --> 01:16:30.410
And when you're
evaluating things,

01:16:30.410 --> 01:16:32.960
just make sure to
not use something

01:16:32.960 --> 01:16:34.190
from the training data.

01:16:34.190 --> 01:16:36.770
Otherwise, your results
