01:18:42.380 --> 01:18:43.880
oh, yeah, one thing,
just quick note

01:18:43.880 --> 01:18:46.820
is that when you're trying
to evaluate these things,

01:18:46.820 --> 01:18:48.830
you know that arithmetic
coding will give you

01:18:48.830 --> 01:18:50.420
this log 1 by P thing.

01:18:50.420 --> 01:18:53.390
So oftentimes, we don't
even do arithmetic coding.

01:18:53.390 --> 01:18:56.660
We just calculate
summation of log 1 by P,

01:18:56.660 --> 01:18:59.115
and we assume that's what
arithmetic coding is going

01:18:59.115 --> 01:19:01.490
to give us because it has a
2 byte overhead or something,

01:19:01.490 --> 01:19:03.320
2 bit overhead.

01:19:03.320 --> 01:19:06.642
So that's just a useful
thing to in practice.

01:19:06.642 --> 01:19:08.600
You don't actually need
to do arithmetic coding

01:19:08.600 --> 01:19:11.270
to know how well your compressor
or predictor is performing,

01:19:11.270 --> 01:19:14.030
just compute the log loss.

